    "https://apply.interfolio.com/190550": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Professor of Law",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: Law School Location: Nashville, TN Vanderbilt University Law School seeks applicants, at the entry or lateral level, in any field of study whose scholarship will help meet our needs. We are seeking to hire multiple tenured/tenure-track faculty in fields including including corporate law, intellectual property, administrative law, health law or public law. Applicants should possess strong academic and professional qualifications. Lateral applicants must have a substantial record of legal scholarship and effective teaching skills. The final candidate for this position must complete a background check. Ideal candidate will possess strong academic and professional qualifications and be able to teach across legal fields and at the intersection of law and other academic disciplines. Lateral applicants must have a substantial record of legal scholarship and effective teaching skills. Educational Requirement:  J.D., Ph.D. or terminal degree Please apply only through this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/190550. We cannot accept applications not submitted using this link. Applications through this link should include a cover letter, resume, research agenda, teaching statement (if applicable), and a minimum of three references. Applications are considered and interviews are scheduled on a rolling basis until the position is filled or the application window closes."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/153471": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Position - Associate or Full Professor",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: School of Nursing Location: Nashville, TN The Vanderbilt University School of Nursing (VUSN) invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Associate or Full Professor. We seek candidates using innovative approaches to address important and challenging problems in all aspects of nursing science. Candidates are expected to have a strong externally funded program of research and participate in the education of graduate students and postdoctoral scientists. We are especially interested in investigators who complement current VUSN and institutional research strengths. Full-time faculty must be willing to relocate to the area for regular, on-campus engagement in VUSN activities. All applications are welcome. However, we currently have needs in the following priority areas: HIV/AIDS, health equity, social determinants of health, health services, global health, community-based research, data, and implementation science. In addition, as an integral part of the Vanderbilt School of Nursing, we have interdisciplinary initiatives in: Acute and Chronic Illness (Pain Management; Cardiovascular; Diabetes; Cancer; Sleep; Substance Use Disorders) and Maternal Child Health (Pregnancy Outcomes; Mother, Infant, Child, and Family Health). The school provides many interdisciplinary collaborative opportunities, including with colleagues in the Vanderbilt University Medical Center and other schools within Vanderbilt University. PhD or equivalent research doctorate. Ability to obtain an unencumbered license to practice as a registered professional nurse (RN) in Tennessee. An established competitive program of research. Recent history of securing external funding. A sustained record of publications in peer-reviewed multidisciplinary and nursing journals. 7-10 years of teaching experience at the graduate level. Evidence of service to the profession, nationally and internationally. Salary and rank will be commensurate with experience. Applicants for the faculty position should submit a current curriculum vitae, cover letter (addressed to Dean Pamela Jeffries), research statement, and contact information for three references. All inquiries are confidential. For questions related to the position, please contact vusnfacultyrecruitment@vanderbilt.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/185901": {
      "title": "Simulation Nurse Educator",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: School of Nursing Location: Nashville This is a full-time, (12-month) faculty position with expectations to be on campus five days per week. The Simulation Nurse Educator is a member of the Simulation Center faculty and staff at Vanderbilt University School of Nursing (VUSN) and provides essential support for healthcare simulation and experiential learning facilitation for Master’s of Nursing (MN), Post-Master’s Certificate and Master’s of Science in Nursing students. Primary responsibilities include developing and facilitating simulations, nursing skills, and OSCEs. Reporting directly to the Assistant Dean for Simulation and Experiential Learning, the Simulation Nurse Educator works collaboratively with faculty, staff, and students to ensure successful learning experiences. Key Functions and Expected Performance Includes: Simulation Design (25%) Collaborates with Program Directors and faculty in simulation selection and design of new simulations based on desired objectives and competencies. Reviews existing simulations annually to ensure they represent current evidence-based practice. Develops simulations using the VUSN template based on identified needs using the Healthcare Simulation Standards of Best Practice and current evidence-based practice. Works with the Director of Simulation Innovation and Extended Reality to incorporate innovative technology, standardized patients, and embedded participants into simulations. Simulation, Skills and OSCE Facilitation (50%) Practice simulations prior to use with students to ensure proficiency with facilitation. Assists Simulation Operations Technicians with setting up and taking down the equipment and supplies as needed. Prepares and executes prebriefing In collaboration with the Program Director and faculty, designs prepwork Delivers a briefing before each simulation or OSCE experience Overview of experience Orientation to the facilities and equipment Acknowledgment of safety and expectations Facilitates the simulation using the Healthcare Simulation Standards of Best Practice Assigns roles to students. Ensures observers are in the correct location and can see and hear the simulation. Carefully observes the simulation, noting correct and incorrect aspects. Guides the Simulation Operations Technicians in managing the fidelity and the responses based on the participant’s actions and the simulation template. Facilitates debriefing Ensures observers and participants are actively engaged. Provides feedback and reflection opportunities using the Healthcare Simulation Standards of Best Practice Simulation Assessment and Evaluation (10%) Collaborates with Program Directors and faculty to develop assessment and evaluation rubrics and tools. Utilizes assessment and evaluation rubrics and tools as directed. Collates data from assessment and evaluation rubrics and tools as directed. Tours & Demonstrations (10%) Conducts lab tours and demonstrations for faculty, students, and external visitors. Additional Responsibilities Performs additional duties as necessary. Job responsibilities listed herein are intended to be a thorough representation of the duties, responsibilities, expectations, and qualifications for this role at the time of hire, but should not be considered all-encompassing, as responsibilities may evolve based on organizational and operational needs. Master’s degree in nursing is required. Unencumbered Registered Nurse licensure in the state of Tennessee is required. Knowledge of the Healthcare Standards of Simulation Best Practice is required. Certification as a Healthcare Simulation Educator (CHSE) is preferred. At least two years' employment as a Registered Nurse or equivalent nursing experience is required One year experience with healthcare simulation is required. Applicants for the faculty position should initially submit a cover letter and resume or curriculum vitae. The cover letter should summarize your qualifications for the position. For questions related to the posting, please email vusnfacultyrecruitment@vanderbilt.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193454": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Modern Chinese Literature and New Media",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Humanities: Asian Studies Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN The Department of Asian Studies at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a tenure-track position in modern Chinese literature and new media. The ideal candidate will have a strong grounding in modern Chinese literature, along with scholarly engagement in at least one area of new media—which may include but is not limited to digital humanities and AI, social media, or the cultural politics of technology. Expertise in Sinophone Studies is welcome. We seek candidates whose teaching can span introductory surveys of modern Chinese literature and advanced thematic courses in areas of specialization and student interest. This position will also support the Arts & Science Core curriculum. Applicants must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. in Asian Studies, comparative literature, or another related field no later than August 1, 2027. Evidence of effective teaching is essential. To be considered, submit a letter of application, CV, research statement, writing sample (an article or chapter), three confidential letters of recommendation, and teaching dossier (teaching statement, student evaluations, and two sample syllabi) by 15 November 2026. Please contact ben.tran@vanderbilt.edu with questions about the position."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193520": {
      "title": "Mellon Assistant Professor of Modern Japanese History",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Humanities: Asian Studies Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN The Department of Asian Studies at Vanderbilt University seeks a Mellon Assistant Professor in Modern Japanese history starting Fall 2027. This non-tenure-track position is for a three-year term with a 2-2 teaching load. We invite candidates who situate the history of modern Japan, 19th-21st centuries, within a regional and/or global framework (such as colonialism and empire, scientific, technological, or cultural exchange, and immigration and diaspora). We also seek candidates whose teaching can span introductory surveys of premodern and modern Japan as well as advanced thematic courses in areas ofspecialization and student interest. This position will also support the Arts & Science Core curriculum. Other responsibilities may include advising students, managing departmental and program extracurricular activities, contributing to programming, and other duties as assigned. To be eligible, candidates must have completed the Ph.D. in a relevant field no longer than four years prior to the appointment date of August 16, 2027, and must have completed all requirements for a Ph.D. before August 1, 2027, in Asian Studies, history, or another related field. Evidence of effective teaching is essential. To be considered, submit a letter of application, CV, writing sample (an article or chapter), three confidential letters of recommendation, and teaching dossier (teaching statement, student evaluations, and two sample syllabi) by 15 December 2026. Please contact ben.tran@vanderbilt.edu with questions about the position."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194557": {
      "title": "Associate Professor of Global Modernism",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Humanities: English Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN The English Department at Vanderbilt University is accepting applications for a tenured Associate Professor in the field of Global Modernism. We especially welcome candidates who study Anglophone modernisms comparatively alongside other modernist traditions of the early 20th century. All faculty in the English Department teach classes at all levels of the curriculum (ranging from introductory and more specialized courses to graduate seminars), participate in the A&S College Core, and serve in an advisory/mentoring capacity for honors students, undergraduate majors, and graduate students. Successful applicants must meet Vanderbilt’s standard for tenure and promotion, which includes a strong record of publication of scholarship of such high quality as to gain favorable recognition within their discipline and at a national level, highly effective teaching, and service to the field. To be considered, submit a letter of application, CV, a research statement, a teaching statement, and a writing sample that reflects current research (an article or chapter) by November 15, 2026. Please contact Prof. Anthony Reed  (anthony.reed@Vanderbilt.Edu) with questions about the position."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193266": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of the Practice of History of Art and Architecture in the field of Global Contemporary Art History",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Humanities: History of Art and Architecture Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN The Department of History of Art and Architecture at Vanderbilt University in Nashville, Tennessee invites applications for a full-time, non-tenure track position of Assistant Professor of the Practice of Global Contemporary Art History. This is a 3-year renewable appointment, conditional upon performance and curricular need, to begin in August 2027. We seek a dynamic teacher-scholar who specializes in late 20th and 21st century art. The position’s geographical focus is open with a preference for candidates whose teaching and research expand beyond the department’s existing strengths in modern European and U.S. art history. We welcome scholars who also maintain concurrent critical, curatorial, or studio practices. We are looking for a colleague who, in addition to or in combination with the above, has the vision and knowledge to develop and teach courses on the contemporary art market that contribute to our new interdisciplinary minor in Museum and Market studies. For current Faculty research strengths and course offerings please visit the departmental website: https://as.vanderbilt.edu/history-art-architecture/ . This position will also support the Arts & Science Core curriculum, with the opportunity to teach in the first-year Core or develop an Exploratory Core course. The position has a teaching load of 3-3 with 1 annual course release for administrative support of our Museums and Markets minor and departmental programming and pre-professional advising. The candidate will be expected to teach a combination of introductory surveys, area specific seminars, and contribute to the College of Arts and Sciences' CORE curriculum. The offering of travel-embedded courses is possible. The nature of the administrative service will be shaped in part by the successful candidate’s skills, interests, and experience, as well as departmental needs. There are opportunities to engage with initiatives across campus including the Engine for Art, Democracy and Justice (Engine for Art, Democracy and Justice | Vanderbilt Uiversity), the Center for Latin American, Caribbean, and Latinx Studies, the Vanderbilt Museum of Art, the Curb Center for Art, Enterprise, and Public Policy, as well as Vanderbilt’s campuses in New York City and San Francisco. A Ph.D. in Art History, Visual Studies, or a related field is required in hand no later than August 1, 2027; teaching experience preferred. Applications should include a cover letter; a CV; writing sample; statement of teaching philosophy (and evidence of effectiveness including recent teaching evaluations); and three confidential letters of reference submitted by the recommender to Interfolio. Review of applications will begin November 15th, 2026. Completed applications received by that date are guaranteed full consideration, and review of applications will continue until the position is filled. For questions regarding this position, please email the chair of the search, Rebecca VanDiver (rebecca.vandiver@vanderbilt.edu)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193839": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor of Nineteenth Century Art",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Humanities: History of Art and Architecture Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN The Department of History of Art and Architecture at Vanderbilt University in Nashville, Tennessee invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track position Assistant Professor of Nineteenth-Century Art History to begin in August 2027. We seek a candidate whose research and teaching consider nineteenth-century art in transnational contexts with a preference for candidates whose teaching and research complement the department’s existing strengths. In addition to outstanding scholarly potential, successful candidates will demonstrate their readiness to contribute to our undergraduate majors, our master’s degree programs, and our new interdisciplinary minor and graduate certificate in Museum and Market studies. For current faculty research strengths and course offerings please visit the departmental website: https://as.vanderbilt.edu/history-art-architecture/. With a teaching load of 2-2, the candidate will be expected to teach a combination of introductory surveys (e.g. Nineteenth-Century Art, History of Art Renaissance to the Present) and area-specific courses. This position will also support the Arts & Science Core curriculum . Within our new Museum and Markets programs, candidates should be prepared to offer a course on the nineteenth-century art market, museum culture, or the gallery system. Ideas for travelembedded courses are welcome and generous funding is available. There are opportunities to engage with initiatives across campus including the Engine for Art, Democracy and Justice, the Center for Latin American, Caribbean, and Latinx Studies, the Vanderbilt Museum of Art, the W.T. Bandy Center for Baudelaire and Modern French Studies, the Robert Penn Warren Center for the Humanities and as well as Vanderbilt’s campuses in New York city and San Francisco. A PhD in Art History, Visual Studies, or a related field must be in hand no later than August 1, 2027. Applications should include a cover letter; a CV; writing sample of no more than 25 pages; statement of teaching philosophy (and evidence of effectiveness including recent teaching evaluations). Letters of recommendation will be solicited from short-listed candidates. Review of applications will begin November 15th, 2026. Completed applications received by that date are guaranteed full consideration, and review of applications will continue until the position is filled. For questions regarding this position, please email the chair of the search, Rebecca VanDiver (rebecca.vandiver@vanderbilt.edu)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/175880": {
      "title": "Tenured Associate or Full Professor of Philosophy",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Humanities: Philosophy Location: Vanderbilt University in Nashville, TN The Vanderbilt Department of Philosophy seeks to hire a full-time tenured faculty member at the rank of associate or full professor.  AOS: Bioethics. AOC: Open.  The position will start in the fall semester of 2026. The typical course load is four courses/year (2/2), including undergraduate and graduate teaching, with standard expectations for service to the department and university. Applicants must have a record of internationally recognized leading-edge scholarship in their respective fields. Ph.D. in Philosophy required. Applicants should send a letter of application, a CV, a statement of research and a teaching statement. Finalists will be asked to supply letters of recommendation and student evaluations. In order to ensure full consideration, please submit materials by November 24, 2025 via Interfolio. Initial interviews will be conducted via Zoom."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194619": {
      "title": "OPEN-RANK FACULTY POSITION in Theoretical Biology",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Natural Sciences: Biological Sciences Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, Tennessee The Department of Biological Sciences seeks an outstanding individual for an open rank faculty position in Theoretical Biology. Theory guides experimental biology by bridging conceptual gaps and distilling signal from distracting biological noise into elegant frameworks, facilitating discovery of new fundamental rules of life. We are thus seeking a faculty member who can use the toolboxes of theoretical biology and applied mathematics to develop an independent and rigorous research program in areas such as evolutionary biology, ecology, epidemiology, genetics, neuroscience or cell and molecular biology.  The successful candidate will be expected to help recruit, teach, and train a robust cohort of undergraduate and graduate students in quantitative biology, collaborate synergistically with other faculty at Vanderbilt, and contribute to the distinction of the University. Applicants must have earned a Ph.D., no later than August 1, 2026, and preferably have one or more years of postdoctoral research experience. Top candidates will demonstrate excellence in research, highly effective teaching and mentoring to undergraduate and graduate students, and a desire to contribute to a collaborative academic community. Candidates should apply via Interfolio by submitting (a) a letter of interest, (b) full curriculum vitae, (c) statement of research interests, and (d) a statement of teaching and mentoring philosophy. Applicants should upload evidence of teaching effectiveness, including course evaluations (if available). Applicants at the assistant professor level should arrange for at least three confidential letters of recommendation to be uploaded to Interfolio. Applicants at higher rank should provide a list of three recommenders in their cover letter. For full consideration, all requested materials should be received by November 6, 2026"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193980": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer (Physical Chemistry)",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Natural Sciences: Chemistry Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, Tennessee The Department Chemistry at Vanderbilt University seeks a highly motivated candidate with a strong commitment to undergraduate education and a Ph.D. in Chemistry to fill a non-tenure track, instructional faculty position at the rank of Senior Lecturer beginning in AY 2027-2028. The ideal candidate will have expertise in the field of physical chemistry and is interested in the instruction of physical and general chemistry, including related laboratory courses. This position will also support the Arts & Science Core curriculum. Other responsibilities may include advising students, managing departmental and program extracurricular activities, contributing to programming, and other duties as assigned. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Chemistry by August 1, 2027. Postdoctoral research and/or teaching experience is desirable. Applicants should highlight their experience working with undergraduates and mentoring undergraduate researchers in the laboratory where possible. Interested individuals should submit the following application materials: cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching evaluations, and a one-page statement of teaching interests and philosophy via Interfolio here: [ https://apply.interfolio.com/193980 ]. The candidate should arrange for at least three reference letters to be submitted on their behalf. Screening of applicants will begin immediately."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194188": {
      "title": "Associate Professor of Experimental Physical Chemistry",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Natural Sciences: Chemistry Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, Tennessee The Department of Chemistry at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a position as a tenured Associate Professor in the area of experimental physical chemistry. Candidates must have a Ph.D. in Chemistry, with a preference for applicants with a record of excellence in time-resolved experimental physical chemistry methods. Research areas of particular interest include nanomaterials, energy, catalysis, electronics and photonics, biology and medicine, and quantum. Vanderbilt Chemistry sits at the center of a highly interdisciplinary research climate and we seek candidates with a collaborative mindset. The successful candidate will have already developed a nationally-recognized research program that complements existing expertise at Vanderbilt University. A strong commitment to research, mentoring, and teaching is expected of any candidate. Vanderbilt University is a private university located on 330 park-like acres on the western edge of downtown Nashville, Tennessee. Nashville is a national center for culture and technology which has been growing alongside the University since Vanderbilt’s founding in 1873. Applicants should submit a cover letter of interest, a curriculum vitae, a research statement, a teaching statement, evidence of teaching effectiveness, and arrange for three letters of recommendation. Application materials must be submitted electronically through [ https://apply.interfolio.com/194188 ] . Review of applications is expected to begin October 15 th , 2026, and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/172996": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor of Mathematics",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Natural Sciences: Mathematics Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN The Department of Mathematics at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a Tenure-Track Assistant Professor position to begin in Fall 2026. All areas of mathematics will be considered. Applicants should demonstrate strong research accomplishments and outstanding promise in both research and teaching. Vanderbilt University is distinguished by its tradition of academic excellence and its culture of collaboration and collegiality. The Department of Mathematics is recognized for its foundational research in pure mathematics as well as for its active engagement in interdisciplinary initiatives across the university's institutes and centers. The department is located at the center of Vanderbilt's park-like campus, in the heart of the growing and thriving city of Nashville. For more information, please visit: https://as.vanderbilt.edu/math/ . Applicants must hold a Ph.D. (in hand no later than August 1, 2026) in Mathematics, Computer Science, or a closely related field, and must have a distinguished record of research, excellence in teaching at both the undergradaute and graduate levels, and demonstrated leadership within their discipline. Applications will be accepted on MathJobs.org (https://www.mathjobs.org/jobs/list/26981) and should include the AMS cover sheet, a cover letter, a CV, a research statement, a teaching statement and all available course evaluations. Finalists will be asked for four letters of reference (including one that specifically addresses teaching). To receive full consideration, applications should be submitted by October 1. After that date, applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. Initial interviews will be conducted via Zoom."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193505": {
      "title": "Tenured Associate Professor of Mathematics",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Natural Sciences: Mathematics Location: Vanderbilt Univeristy, Nashville TN The Department of Mathematics at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a tenured Associate Professor position to begin in Fall 2027. All areas of mathematics will be considered. Applicants should be internationally recognized for their research and have demonstrated excellence in teaching. Vanderbilt University is distinguished by its tradition of academic excellence and its culture of collaboration and collegiality. The Department of Mathematics is recognized for its foundational research in pure and applied mathematics as well as for its active engagement in interdisciplinary initiatives across the university's institutes and centers. The department is located on Vanderbilt's park-like campus, in the heart of the growing and thriving city of Nashville. For more information, please visit: https://as.vanderbilt.edu/math/ Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Mathematics or a closely related field, and must have a distinguished record of research, excellence in teaching at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, and demonstrated leadership within their discipline. This position will also support the Arts & Science Core curriculum. Application Instructions Applications will be accepted on MathJobs.org and should include the AMS cover sheet, a cover letter, a CV, a research statement, a teaching statement, evidence of teaching effectiveness, and four confidential letters of recommendation. We will consider applications on a rolling basis but encourage prospective applicants to apply by October 1, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/172693": {
      "title": "Open-Rank Tenured Faculty Position in Theoretical Quantum Computing and Quantum Materials",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Natural Sciences: Physics & Astronomy Location: Vanderbilt University in Nashville, TN Vanderbilt University’s Departments of Physics & Astronomy and Computer Science jointly invite applications for an open-rank tenured faculty position in the field of theoretical quantum computing and quantum materials. The primary research focus area will be quantum materials by design, emphasizing quantum computation, quantum optics, quantum sensing, and quantum information. The successful applicant will utilize and advance AI methodologies to tackle complex challenges in quantum system simulations and quantum computation. The search will be conducted jointly with the College of Connected Computing. Collaboration with quantum materials researchers from the College of Arts and Science and the School of Engineering, as well as computer and data scientists in the College of Connected Computing is strongly encouraged. The candidate is also expected to pursue active partnership with the Quantum Computing Institute and Center of Nanophase Materials at Oak Ridge National Laboratory. Applications should include: Cover letter a full curriculum vitae. a research statement describing research highlights and future plans. a teaching statement that addresses classroom instruction and mentorship of students and postdoctoral scholars. Review of applications will begin November 15, 2025 and will continue until the position is filled. For questions regarding this posting please email:  kalman.varga@vanderbilt.edu Vanderbilt University is a private research university ranked in the top 20 nationally; its 330-acre park-like campus is located on the western edge of downtown Nashville, TN, known colloquially as Music City and consistently rated as one of the best cities in the US in which to live and work. A Ph.D in physics or closely related field. Applicants are expected to have demonstrated potential for excellence in research, professional leadership, teaching at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, and mentoring. Application materials required: (1) a Cover Letter, (2) a complete CV including published works and preprints, (3) a Research Statement, and (4) a Teaching Statement. Questions can be directed to kalman.varga@vanderbilt.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/173328": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Gravitational‑Wave & Multi‑Messenger Astrophysics",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Natural Sciences: Physics & Astronomy Location: Vanderbilt University in Nashville, TN The Department of Physics & Astronomy at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a tenure‑track Assistant Professor in gravitational‑wave and multi‑messenger astrophysics. We seek an outstanding scholar whose research spans gravitational‑wave theory, data analysis, and/or multi‑messenger astrophysics, ideally grounded in gravitational physics and relativistic astrophysics. Vanderbilt has growing strength, leadership, and strategic momentum in gravitational‑wave science (e.g., in PTAs, LISA, and LIGO-Virgo-KAGRA) and multi‑messenger astrophysics (e.g., in SDSS, LSST, and UVEX), with collaborative ties across Physics & Astronomy, Mathematics, Data Science, and campus‑wide initiatives. Vanderbilt has also recently established the College of Connected Computing, its first new college in 40 years, to advance breakthrough discoveries and strengthen computing education for students through a “computing for all” approach. The candidate will be expected to develop a visible, high-impact, externally funded research program in gravitational‑wave and/or multi‑messenger astrophysics; engage collaboratively with national and international efforts (e.g., PTAs, LISA, LIGO-Virgo-KAGRA) and with Vanderbilt’s broader research efforts, and computational and data‑intensive ecosystem. Candidates will be expected to teach at both undergraduate and graduate levels, advise PhD students, and mentor undergraduate students. They should also contribute to the department, college, and profession, including activities that promote a welcoming and collaborative academic community. Qualifications ● Ph.D. in Physics, Astronomy/Astrophysics, or a closely related field by August 1, 2026 . Preferably with several years of postdoctoral research experience. ● Evidence of research excellence and potential for leadership in gravitational‑wave and/or multi‑messenger astrophysics. ● Commitment to effective teaching and mentoring at both undergraduate and graduate levels. ● Ability to collaborate across subfields of physics, astronomy, mathematics, and data/computational science. Please submit the following: Cover letter describing the candidate's reasons for applying, and their fit to the position; Curriculum vitae with publication list. If relevant, the latter should be separated into publications for which the candidate made major contributions or led, then other large collaborative publications; Research statement, summarizing past achievements and 3–5 year vision, including funding plans, strategy for interdisciplinary collaboration, and complementarity with existing departmental research; Teaching and mentoring statement, addressing (where relevant) the applicant’s approach to classroom and laboratory instruction, mentorship of students and postdoctoral scholars, and ways to foster a welcoming environment for the development of scholars who come to Vanderbilt with many different backgrounds and experiences. Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., course evaluations) must be included, if available; Applicants should arrange for three letters of confidential recommendation to be uploaded via the same link. Review of applications will begin November 1, 2025 , and will continue until the position is filled. For full consideration, please ensure all materials (including letters) are submitted by November 15, 2025 . Inquiries may be directed to Professor Stephen Taylor:  stephen.r.taylor@vanderbilt.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193598": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in High-Energy Nuclear Theory",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Natural Sciences: Physics & Astronomy Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN Position Overview The Department of Physics & Astronomy at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in high-energy nuclear theory . We are particularly interested in candidates addressing fundamental questions about strongly interacting matter, from the emergence of collective behavior to the short-distance structure and dynamics of dense QCD matter. Areas of interest include QCD at finite temperature and density, systems near critical points and far from equilibrium, and probes of QCD matter across multiple scales. Approaches may include effective field theory and perturbative QCD, relativistic hydrodynamics and transport, lattice field theory, and quantum simulations. The successful candidate will join a vibrant high-energy nuclear physics community at Vanderbilt, including a strong experimental program, offering significant opportunities for intellectual exchange across nuclear physics and related fields. Responsibilities Establish and lead an independent, externally funded research program; Contribute to undergraduate and graduate teaching in physics; S upport the Arts & Science Core curriculum; Mentor graduate students and postdoctoral scholars; Engage with Vanderbilt’s interdisciplinary research community and external partners, including Oak Ridge National Laboratory. • Ph.D. in Physics or a closely related field, completed no later than August 1, 2027; • Demonstrated research excellence in theoretical physics, with expertise in one or more areas represented in the search; • A strong research program addressing important questions in high-energy nuclear theory; • Evidence of, or clear potential for, securing external research funding; • Commitment to excellence in teaching and mentorship at all levels. Application Materials Interested candidates should submit the following as a single PDF, or as specified by the application portal: 1. Cover letter; 2. Curriculum vitae, including a complete publication list; 3. Research statement (maximum 3 pages) summarizing key contributions and future research directions; 4. Teaching statement addressing classroom philosophy, curriculum development, and student/postdoctoral mentorship. Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., course evaluations) must be included, if available; 5. Applicants should arrange for three letters of confidential recommendation to be uploaded via the same link. How to Apply Applications should be submitted via https://apply.interfolio.com/193598 . Review Timeline: Review of applications will begin November 1 st , 2026 and continue until the position is filled. For full consideration, please ensure all materials (including letters) are submitted by October 31 st , 2026. Inquiries should be addressed to Professor Sait: sait.a.umar@vanderbilt.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194448": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in Physics",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Natural Sciences: Physics & Astronomy Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN Vanderbilt University’s Department of Physics & Astronomy is inviting applications for a full-time Senior Lecturer position to join a productive and collegial department within a vibrant liberal arts college starting August 2027.  We are seeking a dedicated and student-focused educator capable of teaching across the full range of the undergraduate physics curriculum, including first-year physics, upper-level physics, and advanced laboratory courses. This position will also support the Arts & Science Core curriculum. Other responsibilities may include advising students, managing departmental and program extracurricular activities, contributing to programming, and other duties as assigned. Applicants should hold a Ph.D. in physics, awarded no later than August 1, 2027, and have a demonstrated commitment to effective teaching.  The Senior Lecturer appointment is non-tenure track, with an initial appointment term of one year, renewable to a three-year term subject to satisfactory performance and curricular need.  The teaching load is 3 courses per semester. Applications must include: 1. Full curriculum vitae 2. A statement of teaching philosophy and interests. 3. Teaching evaluation data (1-2 years) and any other evidence of teaching effectiveness 4. Applicants should arrange for three letters of recommendation to also be uploaded via the application link below. These materials should be submitted directly to https://apply.interfolio.com/194448 Review of applications will begin February 15, 2027, and will continue until the position is filled.  For questions regarding this posting please email:  robert.scherrer@vanderbilt.edu Vanderbilt University is a private research university ranked in the top 20 nationally; its 330-acre park-like campus is located on the western edge of downtown Nashville, TN, known colloquially as Music City and consistently rated as one of the best cities in the US in which to live and work."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195247": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Position in Quantum Computing and Simulation",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Natural Sciences: Physics & Astronomy Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN Position Overview The Department of Physics & Astronomy at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Quantum Computing and Simulation.  We invite candidates working across the full quantum information science frontier — whether pursuing foundational theory, developing powerful computational and simulation methods, or forging direct connections to emerging quantum technologies and physical platforms. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to: quantum many-body physics; quantum simulation of field theories and strongly correlated systems; theoretical quantum optics; quantum sensing and metrology; quantum communication and networks; quantum information and computation; quantum algorithms and complexity; quantum error correction and fault-tolerant quantum computing; and quantum architectures and resource estimation. The ideal candidate will bring a perspective that enriches, complements and connects the department's existing research strengths. The Department of Physics & Astronomy hosts vibrant programs in condensed matter physics, astrophysics, biophysics, and high-energy and nuclear physics, with large-scale computational modeling and simulation playing a central role in discovery across these areas. We seek a colleague whose work creates a coherent quantum computational and information-theoretic thread among these fields—not as an isolated specialization, but as an intellectual bridge that may foster collaboration across the department and beyond, including with Oak Ridge National Laboratory and the newly established Vanderbilt Institute for Quantum Innovation at Chattanooga. Responsibilities •    Establish and lead an independent, externally funded research program; •    Contribute to undergraduate and graduate teaching in physics; •    Support the Arts & Science Core curriculum; •    Mentor graduate students and postdoctoral scholars; •    Engage with Vanderbilt's interdisciplinary research community and external partners, including Oak Ridge National Laboratory and the Vanderbilt Institute for Quantum Innovation at Chattanooga. ·      Ph.D. in Physics or a closely related field, awarded no later than August 1, 2027; ·      Demonstrated research excellence in theoretical physics, with expertise in one or more areas represented in the search; ·      A strong research program addressing important questions in quantum computing and simulation; ·      Evidence of, or clear potential for, securing external research funding; ·      Commitment to excellence in teaching and mentorship at all levels. Application Materials Interested candidates should submit the following as a single PDF, or as specified by the application portal : 1.    Cover letter 2.    Curriculum vitae, including a complete publication list 3.    Research statement (maximum 3 pages) summarizing key contributions and future research directions 4.    Teaching statement addressing classroom philosophy, curriculum development, and student/postdoctoral mentorship. Evidence of teaching effectiveness (e.g., course evaluations) must be included, if available 5.    Applicants should arrange for three confidential letters of recommendation to be uploaded via the same link How to Apply Applications should be submitted via [ https://apply.interfolio.com/195247 ]. Review Timeline: Review of applications will begin December 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled.  For full consideration, please ensure all materials (including letters) are submitted by November 30, 2026.  Inquiries should be addressed to Professor Kalman Varga at kalman.varga@vanderbilt.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/173316": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Clinical Psychology - Tenure Track",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Natural Sciences: Psychology Location: Vanderbilt University in Nashville, TN The Clinical Psychology Program in the Department of Psychology of the College of Arts & Science at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a tenure-track position as an Assistant Professor in Clinical Psychology. The Vanderbilt Clinical Psychology Program is internationally recognized for its strength in experimental psychopathology, affective science, intervention research, and clinical neuroscience. The Department of Psychology, housed within the College of Arts and Science, is a community of 27 full-time faculty with a strong commitment to excellence in research and teaching in psychology and neuroscience. Our faculty are organized into three overlapping areas representing clinical science, cognition and cognitive neuroscience, and neuroscience. We have strong collaborative relations with the Department of Psychology and Human Development in Peabody College at Vanderbilt and with the Department of Psychiatry and other departments affiliated with the Vanderbilt University Medical Center . For information about the faculty, department, and interdisciplinary opportunities, please see https://as.vanderbilt.edu/psychology/ . Vanderbilt University is a top ranked private research university. Its 330-acre park-like campus is located on the western edge of downtown Nashville, TN, known colloquially as Music City and consistently rated as one of the best cities in the US in which to live and work. We seek an exceptional individual who has a strong record of creative, methodologically rigorous, and conceptually driven research, who has clear potential for obtaining extramural funding, and who has demonstrated the potential for effective teaching at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. Although individuals from all areas of adult clinical psychology will be considered, we are particularly interested in scientists who will complement or expand on the expertise of our current clinical faculty. We have particular interest in applicants with expertise in the development and implementation of evidence-based treatments for psychopathology using multi-method approaches and/or the ability to conduct translational research that uses multi-method approaches and paradigms from the fields of affective science to inform the study of psychopathology. A Ph.D., awarded no later than August 1, 2026, in Clinical Psychology from an APA- or PCSAS-accredited clinical program is required; completion of an APA-accredited internship is preferred but not required. Applicants should submit: (a) a cover letter, (b) a complete curriculum vitae, (c) up to three representative publications, (d) a statement of research and teaching interests, (e) students course evaluations if available, and (f) at least three letters of reference . The cover letter should articulate the applicant's specific interest in this position, explain how this role aligns with their career goals, and/or discuss their fit with our department and institution. Screening and interviewing of applications will begin November 15, 2025. For questions regarding the position, please contact Bunmi Olatunji, Gertrude Conaway Vanderbilt Professor of Psychology, Director of Clinical Training, Department of Psychology (olubunmi.o.olatunji@vanderbilt.edu)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193734": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor, Department of Psychology, Vanderbilt University in Cognition and Cognitive Neuroscience",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Natural Sciences: Psychology Location: Nashville, TN The Department of Psychology in the College of Arts and Science at Vanderbilt University seeks applications for a tenure-track assistant professor position in the areas of cognition and cognitive neuroscience broadly defined. Position to start August 16, 2027. Our department is a community of faculty with a strong commitment to excellence in research and teaching in psychology and neuroscience. Our department’s faculty are organized into three overlapping areas representing clinical science, cognition and cognitive neuroscience, and neuroscience. Our cognition and cognitive neuroscience faculty are internationally recognized for research in cognition, cognitive neuroscience, and vision science, with particular strengths in visual perception, visual cognition, attention, memory, cognitive control, decision making, cognitive and neurocomputational modeling, and related areas. We welcome applications from scientists whose research complements and extends our areas of excellence. The Department of Psychology has strong relations with the Department of Psychology and Human Development at Peabody College, hosting with them a joint PhD program in Psychological Sciences, the Vanderbilt Brain Institute and both the undergraduate and graduate programs in Neuroscience, and the new College of Connected Computing . Our faculty have affiliations with and capitalize on resources from the Vanderbilt University Medical Center , the Academic Computing Center for Research & Education , Vanderbilt Institute of Imaging Science as well as a planned new MEG facility, the Data Science Institute , and the Vanderbilt Kennedy Center for Research on Human Development . For information about the faculty, department, and interdisciplinary opportunities in the Department of Psychology, please see https://as.vanderbilt.edu/psychology/ The successful candidate will have opportunities to teach and supervise undergraduate and graduate students in psychology, neuroscience, and in future programs being developed in collaboration with the College of Connected Computing. This position will also support the Arts & Science Core curriculum. Candidates should be eligible for a tenured-track appointment, have a strong track record of research, and be prepared for teaching in cognition, cognitive neuroscience, and/or vision science. A Ph.D. in Psychology, Neuroscience, or a related discipline, awarded no later than August 1, 2027, is required. Applicants should submit: (a) a curriculum vitae, (b) a research and teaching statement of no more than five pages, (c) up to three relevant publications, (d) a brief cover letter that outlines your interest in the position and your fit for the department, as well as any other relevant information that is not provided in the other documents, directly to https://apply.interfolio.com/193734 . The online application portal (Interfolio) will ask for the names and emails of three references; references will be prompted to submit letters of recommendation via the Interfolio portal. Screening of applications will begin after November 15, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194049": {
      "title": "Open Rank Faculty Position in Clinical Psychology, Department of Psychology, Vanderbilt University",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Natural Sciences: Psychology Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN The Clinical Psychology Program in the Department of Psychology of the College of Arts & Science at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a faculty position in Clinical Psychology at any rank, tenure-track or tenured. The Vanderbilt Clinical Psychology Program is internationally recognized for its strength in experimental psychopathology, affective science, intervention research, and clinical neuroscience. The successful candidate will join the core faculty of our APA- and PCSAS-accredited clinical science training program, which is designed to prepare students for academic/research careers in clinical psychology. The Department of Psychology, housed within the College of Arts and Science, is a community of 27 full-time faculty with a strong commitment to excellence in research and teaching in psychology and neuroscience. Our faculty are organized into three overlapping areas representing clinical science, cognition and cognitive neuroscience, and neuroscience. We have strong collaborative relations with the Department of Psychology and Human Development in Peabody College at Vanderbilt, hosting with them a joint PhD program in Psychological Sciences, and with the Department of Psychiatry and other departments affiliated with the Vanderbilt University Medical Center . For information about our faculty, department, and interdisciplinary opportunities, please see https://as.vanderbilt.edu/psychology/ . Vanderbilt University is a top ranked private research university. Its 330-acre park-like campus is located on the western edge of downtown Nashville, TN, known colloquially as Music City and is consistently rated as one of the best cities in the US in which to live and work. We seek an exceptional individual who has a strong record of creative, methodologically rigorous, and conceptually driven research, and who, depending on rank, has clear potential for or a robust track record of obtaining extramural funding. We welcome applicants with a strong research program in any area of adult clinical psychology, broadly defined. We are especially interested in applicants whose work complements and extends existing strengths in our department, for example those with expertise in the development and implementation of evidence-based treatments for psychopathology using multi-method approaches, and/or those who conduct translational research that uses experimental approaches to inform the study of psychopathology. We welcome applicants with the capacity for teaching at both the graduate and undergraduate levels, and the capacity for mentoring and supporting graduate students and undergraduates in research. This position will also support the Arts & Science Core curriculum. A Ph.D., awarded no later than August 1, 2027, in Clinical Psychology from an APA- or PCSAS-accredited clinical program is required; completion of an APA-accredited internship is preferred but not required. Applicants should submit: (a) a curriculum vitae, (b) a research and teaching statement of no more than five pages, (c) evidence of teaching effectiveness, including recent course evaluations, (d) up to three relevant publications, (e) a brief cover letter that outlines your interest in the position and your fit for the department, as well as any other relevant information that is not provided in the other documents. The online application portal (Interfolio) will ask for the names and emails of three references; references will be prompted to submit letters of recommendation via the Interfolio portal. Screening of applications will begin after November 15, 2026. For questions regarding the position, please contact Bunmi Olatunji, Gertrude Conaway Vanderbilt Professor of Psychology, Director of Clinical Training, Department of Psychology ( olubunmi.o.olatunji@vanderbilt.edu )."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195052": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor, Archaeology of South America",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Social Sciences: Anthropology Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN The Department of Anthropology at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a tenured position at the rank of Associate or Full Professor in the archaeology of South America. We are especially interested in senior scholars working in the Andean region, greater Amazonia, or in the interfaces between these regions. We seek a scholar whose research will extend one of the leading programs in South American archaeology in North America and who will help us build an expansive, continentally connected archaeology rooted in anthropological holism. Vanderbilt’s existing South American cluster spans Ecuador through the south-central Andes, with complementary expertise across coastal, highland, and lowland ecologies and across prehispanic, historical/colonial and bioarchaeological approaches. The successful candidate will join a collaborative department with internationally recognized strengths in Latin Americanist anthropology, trans-disciplinary research, biocultural and forensic anthropology, and computational and spatial methods. We welcome candidates whose interests complement those of our faculty, and especially work that engages thematic clusters such as long-term human–environment dynamics, historical ecology, and landscape transformation; the archaeology of complex societies, urbanism, and imperial formations. Successful candidates will integrate innovative theory and methods which may include archaeometry, geoarchaeology, paleobotany, zooarchaeology, and computational approaches. The teaching load for research-active faculty is 2:1. Courses with experiential or field-based components are particularly welcome. As a senior hire, the successful candidate will bring a sustained record of externally funded fieldwork, high-impact publication, highly effective teaching, and graduate mentorship. They will contribute intellectual leadership to the growth of the department’s undergraduate program, as well as the continued strength of its Ph.D. program. This position will also support the Arts & Science Core curriculum. We especially value candidates whose work connects to other campus units, including with the College of Connected Computing, History of Art and Architecture, Earth and Environmental Sciences, and the Center for Latin American, Caribbean, and Latinx Studies. Applicants should submit through Interfolio: (a) a cover letter describing research, teaching, and community-engagement trajectories; (b) a full curriculum vitae; (c) a research statement; (d) a statement of teaching and mentorship philosophy; and (e) the names and contact information of three references. Review of applications will begin on November 9, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Applications received by November 9 will receive full consideration; later applications will be reviewed as the search proceeds. Candidates are encouraged to apply early."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195057": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor, Archaeology of Mesoamerica",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Social Sciences: Anthropology Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN The Department of Anthropology at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a tenured appointment at the rank of Associate or Full Professor in anthropological archaeology, with a research specialization in Mesoamerica. We seek a scholar with an established international reputation and an active, sustained research program that complements current faculty expertise. We are particularly interested in candidates whose work engages one or more of the following: regional-scale landscape and settlement archaeology, including airborne lidar and other remote-sensing approaches; coupled human-environment systems, historical and political ecology, and human ecodynamics; urbanism, collective action, and political economy; or the archaeology of colonial transformations and long-term indigenous histories. Demonstrated strength in data-intensive analytics — machine learning, geospatial methods, archaeometry, or paleobotany — is desirable. Successful candidates will direct a substantial, ongoing, externally-funded portfolio of field-based, or mixed field-and-laboratory research, long-standing institutional and host-community partnerships, and the capacity to contribute to departmental and program leadership within a dynamic four-field program with deep hemispheric commitments. The appointee will join a growing cluster of faculty working at continental scales on the long-term histories of Indigenous American societies, with opportunities for collaboration with Vanderbilt’s College of Connected Computing, the Data Science Institute, the Vanderbilt Lab for Immersive AI Translation (VALIANT), and the Center for Latin American, Caribbean, and Latinx Studies, among other trans-institutional initiatives. The teaching load for research-active faculty is 2:1. Courses with experiential or field-based components are particularly welcome. As a senior hire, the successful candidate will bring a strong record of externally funded research, high-impact publication, highly effective teaching, and graduate mentorship. They will contribute intellectual leadership to the growth of the department’s undergraduate program and the continued strength of its Ph.D. program. This position will also support the Arts & Science Core curriculum. We especially value candidates whose work will connect to other campus units, including with the College of Connected Computing, History of Art and Architecture, Earth and Environmental Sciences, and the Center for Latin American, Caribbean, and Latinx Studies. Applicants should submit through Interfolio: (a) a cover letter describing research, teaching, and community-engagement trajectories; (b) a full curriculum vitae; (c) a research statement; (d) a statement of teaching and mentorship philosophy; and (e) the names and contact information of three references. Review of applications will begin on November 9, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Start date: August 16, 2027."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193645": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer position in Economics",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Social Sciences: Economics Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville TN The Department of Economics at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a full-time, non-tenure-track Senior Lecturer position to begin August 16, 2027.  A successful candidate will have a PhD. in Economics awarded no later than August 1, 2027, and a strong desire to teach undergraduate courses in core areas of economics as well as the ability to offer elective courses in their fields of interest. The standard course load will be six courses per year and would likely include multiple sections of some courses. This position will also support the Arts & Science Core curriculum. Other responsibilities may include advising students, managing departmental and program extracurricular activities, contributing to programming, and other duties as assigned. The initial term of appointment will be for one to three years with the possibility of renewal contingent upon performance review and curricular need.  Compensation will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Vanderbilt stresses excellence in teaching for non-tenure-track faculty in its undergraduate programs. The position will remain open until filled. To apply, please submit CV, sample research paper, statement of teaching interests, evidence of teaching effectiveness, including one to two years of recent student course evaluations, if available, and three confidential letters of recommendation. Interested applicants should submit their applications via Interfolio at this link: https://apply.interfolio.com/193645 . We will commence reviewing applications on a rolling basis starting January 1, 2027."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195147": {
      "title": "Associate Professor position in labor and public economics",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Social Sciences: Economics Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN, USA The Department of Economics at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a full-time position at the rank of Associate Professor, with tenure, beginning August 16, 2027, in the areas of labor and public economics. Vanderbilt University is committed to excellence in research and teaching across its doctoral, master’s, and undergraduate programs. This position will also support the Arts & Science Core Curriculum. Appointment at the Associate Professor level requires a Ph.D. and a demonstrated record of excellence in research and teaching. Applicants should submit a curriculum vitae, a representative sample of research publications, research and teaching statements, evidence of teaching effectiveness such as recent course evaluations, and the names and contact information of three individuals wno may be asked to provide letters of recommendation. Applications should be submitted through Interfolio at: https://apply.interfolio.com/195147 ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191788": {
      "title": "Mellon Assistant Professor in Russian and Eurasian History",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Humanities: History Location: Nashville, TN  The department of history at Vanderbilt University seeks a Mellon Assistant Professor in Russian and Eurasian History. This non-tenure-track position is for a three-year term with a 2-2 teaching load. The department is especially interested in candidates who have innovative research agendas and whose teaching can cover broad dimensions of Imperial, Soviet, and or/post-Soviet history. All faculty in the History Department teach classes at all levels of the curriculum, from introductory lectures to majors' seminars, participate in the A&S college Core, and serve in an advisory capacity for honors students and undergraduate majors. Applicants must have received their Ph.D. in history no more than four years prior to the start of the position and must have their degree in hand by August 15, 2027. Candidates must have evidence of teaching experience. Applications are accepted via Interfolio. To be considered, please submit the following materials: cover letter, curriculum vitae, one writing sample, three letters of reference, a teaching statement, one sample syllabus, and teaching evaluations for all available courses (at least two sets of teaching evaluations), Review of application files will begin December 1, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191792": {
      "title": "Mellon Assistant Professor in the History of Empire",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Humanities: History Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN The Department of History at Vanderbilt University seeks a Mellon Assistant Professor in the History of Empire starting Fall 2027. This non-tenure-track position is for a three-year term with a 2-2 teaching load. The department is especially interested in candidates who have innovative research agendas in British, French, Spanish, Dutch and or/Portuguese empires, circa 1500-1900. The successful candidate will teach a course in global history and also contribute electives on empire that support one or both of our vibrant interdisciplinary majors: Law, History, and Society and Economics and History. All faculty in the History Department teach classes at all levels of the curriculum, from introductrory lectures to majors' seminars, participate in the A&S college Core, and serve in an advisory capacity for honors students and undergraduate majors. Applicants must have received their Ph.D in history no more than four years prior to the start of the position and must have their degree in hand by August 1, 2027. Candidates must have evidence of teaching experience. Applications are accepted via Interfolio. To be considered, please submit the following materials: cover letter, curriculum vitae, one writing sample, three letters of reference, a teaching statement, one sample syllabus, and teaching evaluations for all available courses (at least two sets of teaching evaluations). Review of applications files will begin on January 11, 2027."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193611": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Global Economic History",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Humanities: History Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN The Department of History at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a tenure-track assistant professor in Global Economic History. We seek a scholar with an excellent research record on any topic within an economic framework, including, but not limited to, political economy; the history of economic institutions and regulatory regimes; the history of economic thought; free and unfree labor; histories of consumption and material culture; the history of capitalism; or the integration of environmental, legal, and economic histories. We welcome all approaches, chronologies, and geographic areas of expertise, but have particular interest in candidates whose research engages regions outside the United States and who are capable of working with and teaching complex datasets and quantitative historical methods. All faculty in the History Department teach classes at all levels of the curriculum, from introductory lectures to majors' seminars, participate in the A&S college core, and serve in an advisory capacity for honors students and undergraduate majors. Applicants should hold a Ph.D. in History or a related field. Candidates should also have evidence of teaching experience. The Ph.D. must be in hand by August 1, 2027. Applications are accepted via Interfolio [ https://apply.interfolio.com/193611 ] . To be considered, please submit the following materials: cover letter, curriculum vitae, two to three writing samples, three confidential letters of reference, a teaching statement, one sample syllabus, and teaching evaluations for all available courses. Review of application files will begin on November 1, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191913": {
      "title": "Part-Time Lecturer in Medicine, Health and Society",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Social Sciences: Medicine, Health and Society Location: Vanderbilt University-New York City Campus  We seek one part-time instructor to join Vanderbilt University’s Department of Medicine, Health, and Society, a large, interdisciplinary major designed to prepare the next generation of national and international health leaders—from doctors and nurses to economists, researchers, and policy makers. This instructor will be appointed to teach at the new Vanderbilt New York City campus, located in Chelsea. This non-tenure-track, part-time Lecturer position is a one-semester, course-based appointment effective Spring 2027 and is renewable each semester, conditional upon performance and need. Duties involve teaching one section of MHS-NYC 2550 What is Health? (and how do you get it)?, supervising student internships, and attending weekly meetings with other instructors. MHS-NYC 2250 is an interdisciplinary exploration of how health is defined, contested, and pursued across social, cultural, and policy domains, including infrastructure, activism, hospitals and clinics, politics, media, and popular discourse. Students use the city as a laboratory to analyze health as a social good, a business, and a moral imperative. A PhD in the humanities, social sciences, an MD or degree in clinical specialties, or a related field is required. Experience with experiential education is preferred. Compensation will be commensurate with qualifications and experience, with the expected salary to be $9,000 per course. Applications, including cover letter, CV, teaching statement, evidence of teaching effectiveness, course evaluations, and list of three references should be submitted. Candidates who advance in the process will be asked to submit three letters of recommendation. Applications will be reviewed as they are received. Questions about the position should be directed to Jonathan Metzl, Jonathan.Metzl@Vanderbilt.edu, Chair of the Department of Medicine, Health, and Society"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194107": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor in Comparative Politics",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Social Sciences: Political Science Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN The Department of Political Science at Vanderbilt University invites applications from outstanding political scientists with a specialization in comparative politics for appointment as Associate Professor or Full Professor with tenure. We are interested in scholars who study any of a range of topics, including political institutions, comparative political economy, conflict and violence, and political behavior. The department is committed to building an intellectually vibrant community of exceptional faculty. Successful candidates will have an excellent record of research and be able to contribute to undergraduate and graduate training in comparative politics. Candidates able to assist in graduate training in quantitative methods, formal theory, or other methods should indicate those competencies. This position will also support the Arts and Science Core curriculum. The appointment will begin Fall 2027. Salary and benefits are highly competitive. Applicants shold submit a cover letter, curriculum vita, research statement, and teaching statement at https://apply.interfolio.com/194107. The Department will begin reviewing files as early as October 15, 2026. To ensure full consideration, applicants should submit all materials by October 30, 2026, but we welcome earlier applications, and we may schedule formal interviews at any time. We will accept applications until we fill the position."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193367": {
      "title": "Tenured Associate Professor of Sociology in field of Computing and Society",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Social Sciences: Sociology Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN The Department of Sociology at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a tenured Associate Professor in Sociology with research specialization in computing and society to begin fall semester 2027. The successful candidate will study the societal drivers and effects of computing as a social and material force, applying sociological theories and methods to questions of AI, machine learning, quantum computing, and/or social media. They will use rigorous and theoretically driven qualitative and/or quantitative methods, and their work will have strong potential for external funding. An ideal candidate will be prepared to respond to growing student demand, both undergraduate and graduate students, to learn about how social factors are built into computing systems and how computing systems organize social life. Additionally, we seek a faculty member who will complement our department’s existing strengths in sociological study of demography, health, social psychology, and/or work. This position will also support the Arts & Science Core curriculum, with the opportunity to teach in the first-year Core or develop an Exploratory Core course. Qualifications: Vanderbilt expects excellence in research and teaching. A Ph.D. in sociology is required. Please submit via Interfolio the following 1) a letter describing research and teaching interests and professional experience 2) curriculum vitae. Shortlisted candidates will be invited to submit three letters of reference and evidence of teaching effectiveness, including course evaluations. Applications will be reviewed starting October 15, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Please contact Holly McCammon ( holly.j.mccammon@vanderbilt.edu ), Department Chair, with any questions."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195322": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Biomedical Engineering",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: School of Engineering: Biomedical Engineering Location: Vanderbilt University Vanderbilt Biomedical Engineering seeks to recruit an Assistant Professor whose scholarship and translational program advance autonomous, sensor-rich, and decision-support-enabled treatment systems for trauma and procedural care in austere, contested, and remote-care environments. The target candidate will develop technologies that extend life-saving intervention capability when expert personnel, infrastructure, and evacuation are limited, conditions central to Large-Scale Combat Operations, Prolonged Casualty Care, and future lunar/exploration-class space missions. Particular emphasis is placed on systems that assess, predict, and actively manage patient physiological status during injury, resuscitation, stabilization, and transport. These challenges are increasingly relevant to distributed national security scenarios and emerging space-health applications, where medical expertise, resources, communications, and evacuation capabilities may all be constrained. The search will prioritize candidates working at the intersection of medical robotics, soft robotic and advanced actuation technologies for medical applications, autonomous therapeutic delivery systems, autonomy and human-machine teaming, intelligent physiological sensing and monitoring, computational physiology, AI-enabled guidance and decision support, adaptive treatment systems, and novel treatment modality end-effectors , with clear pathways to prototypes, validation, and translation. Representative focus areas could include: (1) autonomous or semi-autonomous robotic assistance for casualty stabilization and damage-control procedures; (2) soft robotic, compliant, and bioinspired systems for diagnostic, therapeutic, or interventional applications; (3) mixed and augmented reality for guidance, telepresence, and just-in-time training; (4) sensor fusion, physiological-state estimation, and predictive monitoring for adaptive treatment and resuscitation; (5) closed-loop therapeutic systems that autonomously adjust interventions based on real-time physiological feedback; (6) automated treatments and therapeutic systems for austere, contested, remote, or spaceflight conditions; (7) robust algorithms for operations under limited imaging, uncertain anatomy, physiologic instability, or degraded communications; and (8) deployable platforms engineered for size, weight, power, maintainability, and field reliability. Particularly attractive candidates will develop technologies that advance either intelligent autonomous care systems, novel intervention and therapeutic delivery technologies, or the integration of both approaches , enabling autonomous or semi-autonomous management of hemorrhage, shock, burn and traumatic injury, physiological instability, and other acute conditions encountered when expert clinical care is unavailable. Successful candidates will view the patient as an integral component of the engineered system and develop approaches that explicitly incorporate physiological response, treatment efficacy, and patient-specific adaptation into the design of autonomous and semi-autonomous care technologies. This position is designed to collaborate directly with Vanderbilt's established and rapidly growing ecosystem for operational medicine, autonomy, and frontier health systems. The new faculty member will have immediate engagement opportunities with VU/VUMC collaborators and operational partners , including designated relationships with VUMC Emergency Medicine , the Center for Emergency Care Research and Innovation (CERI) , and the Vanderbilt Institute for Surgery and Engineering (VISE) . The candidate will also have opportunities to engage with Vanderbilt's LUNAR Labs Initiative , which is advancing technologies and operational concepts for sustained human presence in space and other extreme environments. In addition, Vanderbilt's long-standing partnership with Fort Campbell and the 101st Airborne Division provides a rare \"living laboratory\" for co-design, field evaluation, and doctrine-relevant translation, complemented by ties to the Joint Trauma System and a broader network of operational stakeholders and subject-matter experts. Together, these partnerships create a unique environment for developing next-generation technologies capable of delivering trauma, emergency, and procedural care across defense, disaster-response, rural, and space-exploration settings. Candidates with added research concentrations that complement the department's existing strengths in biophotonics, biomedical imaging, medical devices and interventional systems, constrained resources and microfluidic diagnostics, and neural engineering, therapeutic bioengineering are also of interest. U.S. citizenship and eligibility to obtain and maintain a U.S. security clearance are desirable qualifications that may facilitate participation in certain federally sponsored research programs, including projects supported by DARPA and the Department of Defense. These qualifications will be considered positively. Applicants must demonstrate the ability to develop a vibrant, externally funded research program, along with an equal commitment to outstanding teaching at both the undergraduate and graduate levels.  VUBME exists at both the scientific and geographic boundary of Vanderbilt’s engineering and clinical departments of its renowned medical center, making it an ideal location for engineering research at the interface of technology and medicine.  Ideal candidates should possess: a doctorate degree in biomedical engineering or closely related fields of science and engineering, a record of scholarly and research achievement appropriate for their current level, strong record of high-impact research, a commitment to high-quality instruction in biomedical engineering and interest in advancing education programs at both undergraduate and graduate levels, an ability to lead a research program with a dedication to inclusive community building, and an interest in collaboration across engineering and with basic science and clinical disciplines. Application materials must include: 1) cover letter, 2) CV, 3) statement of research interests (of particular import is their goals and collaborative possibilities across Vanderbilt), 4) a statement of teaching philosophy highlighting potential synergies within the BME department, such as new courses, and 5) the names and contact information of at least three professional references. Review will begin upon receipt, and applications received by December 15, 2026 will receive full consideration.  Please address questions to the email: VUBMESearch@vanderbilt.edu ( also note, this email will only be monitored by the chair of the department, and the chair of the search to maintain confidentiality) ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193644": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Professor – Proposal Development & Research Advancement",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: School of Engineering: Civil and Environmental Engineering Location: Nashville, TN The Vanderbilt Center for Sustainability, Energy, and Climate (VSEC) seeks a Research Assistant Professor to provide strategic and operational leadership in research proposal development and external funding advancement. This position will contribute both strategically and intellectually to the development of multidisciplinary research initiatives, in addition to coordinating proposal development and submission. The role will actively support VSEC’s mission by expanding the center’s capacity to identify, develop, and submit competitive proposals for externally sponsored research. The Research Assistant Professor will work closely with the VSEC Director, VSEC Deputy Director, Director of Operations, core faculty, and affiliated researchers to cultivate and coordinate multidisciplinary teams, align faculty expertise with emerging funding opportunities, and advance short- and long-term funding strategies for the center. The position will play a vital role in strengthening VSEC’s research development infrastructure and enhancing its competitiveness for federal, foundation, industry, and other external support. Key responsibilities Identify, assess, and prioritize external funding opportunities that align with VSEC’s research strengths, institutional priorities, and emerging areas of opportunity. D evelop, prepare, and submit competitive proposals to secure external funding for VSEC research projects and center-based initiatives. W ork closely with the VSEC Director, VSEC Deputy Director, and Director of Operations to develop and implement short- and long-term funding strategies. M anage proposal development processes from opportunity identification through final submission, including timelines, internal coordination, sponsor requirements, and submission readiness. D evelop and coordinate VSEC-centered multidisciplinary proposal teams that draw on the expertise of VSEC core faculty, affiliated investigators, research staff, postdoctoral scholars, and external collaborators as appropriate. T ranslate faculty expertise and center capabilities into compelling proposal concepts, white papers, concept notes, and other strategic materials that strengthen the competitiveness of VSEC-led submissions. C oordinate with Vanderbilt’s Office of Sponsored Research, Vanderbilt’s Office of Vice Provost for Research and Innovation, grants management personnel, and other relevant campus partners to facilitate proposal development and ensure timely, compliant submissions. S upport the strategic framing of proposals, including articulation of research significance, innovation, team strengths, and alignment with sponsor priorities. H elp expand VSEC’s overall proposal development capacity and grant activity, particularly for large-scale, interdisciplinary, and multi-investigator opportunities. M aintain awareness of sponsor trends, program priorities, and evolving funding landscapes relevant to energy, sustainability, decarbonization, infrastructure, and related research areas. T rack proposal pipelines, submissions, outcomes, and resubmission opportunities to support continuous improvement in VSEC’s research development efforts. C ontribute, as appropriate, to white papers, publications, workshops, and research planning activities that help advance VSEC’s visibility and long-term research agenda. E ngage in proposal improvement processes to enhance win rates/competitiveness through narrative shaping, positioning, and team composition. L ead and collaborate on research initiatives with VSEC faculty, researchers, and partners, contributing technical expertise to the design, execution, and advancement of research projects. ead and contribute to the development, writing, and publication of scholarly papers, technical reports, and other research outputs. Preferred Qualifications and Attributes PhD in a field relevant to VSEC’s mission and research portfolio. Demonstrated experience in research development, proposal preparation, or management of externally funded academic research initiatives. Strong understanding of the external funding landscape, including federal agencies, foundations, and industry-sponsored research. Experience coordinating multidisciplinary or multi-investigator proposal teams. Ability to translate technical expertise into clear, persuasive, and strategically positioned proposal narratives. Excellent organizational, project management, and communication skills. Ability to work effectively with faculty, research staff, administrators, and external collaborators across disciplines. Familiarity with university pre-award processes, proposal compliance requirements, and sponsor submission expectations. Capacity to contribute both operationally and intellectually to the advancement of center-based research initiatives. Applications should include a full CV, research statement, biography, and 3 reference contacts. Applications should be submitted on-line at: apply.interfolio.com/193644 . For more information, please visit our web sites: https://engineering.vanderbilt.edu/cee/ .  Review of applications will begin immediately, and applications will be accepted until the position is filled. For questions regarding the position, send an email to: g.chapman@vanderbilt.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195517": {
      "title": "Open Rank Tenured/Tenure-track Professor, Department of Mechanical Engineering - Space Technologies",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: School of Engineering: Mechanical Engineering Location: Nashville, TN The Department of Mechanical Engineering at Vanderbilt University invites applications for an open-rank, tenured/tenure-track faculty position to begin in Fall 2027. We are seeking outstanding candidates in mechanical engineering systems for space applications. Examples include, but are not limited to space vehicles design, thermal management in space, space manufacturing, aerospace propulsion, dynamics and control of space systems. This position will also be affiliated with the newly established Vanderbilt Insitute for Innovations Beyond Earth (VIIBE). The successful candidate’s expertise should complement and strengthen existing departmental research in dynamics and control, robotics, thermofluid sciences, materials, and manufacturing. Candidates should have a strong theoretical foundation and a clear vision for applying fundamental mechanical engineering principles to space technologies. The successful candidate is expected to develop a strong, externally funded research program and to pursue excellence in both scholarship and teaching. They should be capable of teaching one or more core mechanical engineering courses and contributing to the expansion of graduate-level offerings. Applicants with expertise in either experimental or computational research are encouraged to apply. Experience with the application of AI and machine-learning tools in research, and the incorporation of these technologies into teaching, will be considered a significant advantage. Applications will be reviewed as they are received; those received by December 1, 2026, will be given full consideration. Applications should include the following materials: Cover letter Complete curriculum vitae Statement of research interests Statement of teaching philosophy Service statement (required for tenured applicants) Contact information for at least three references"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195543": {
      "title": "Open-Rank Tenured/Tenure-Track Professor, Department of Mechanical Engineering – Robotics and Autonomous Systems",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: School of Engineering: Mechanical Engineering Location: Nashville, TN The Department of Mechanical Engineering at Vanderbilt University invites applications for an open-rank, tenured/tenure-track faculty position in robotics and autonomous systems, to begin in Fall 2027. We seek outstanding candidates whose research advances the fundamentals of robotics and autonomy and who are eager to apply that work to pressing challenges in national security and defense. The successful candidate is expected to be an experienced researcher with an established research program and a strong track record of securing external funding. This position is part of an interdisciplinary cluster hire affiliated with the Vanderbilt University Institute of National Security, founded in 2024. The Institute advances its mission by sponsoring research in areas such as AI resilience and cyber defense while connecting Vanderbilt faculty and students with leaders across the military, government, and industry. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to help shape this rapidly growing enterprise. Research areas of interest include, but are not limited to, aerial, underwater, and ground-based autonomous vehicles and wearable robotics for human performance augmentation. The candidate's expertise should complement and strengthen existing departmental strengths in dynamics and control, robotics, thermofluid sciences, materials, and manufacturing, and they should bring a strong theoretical foundation and a clear vision for applying fundamental mechanical engineering principles to problems of national consequence. The successful candidate is expected to develop a strong, externally funded research program and to pursue excellence in both scholarship and teaching. They should be capable of teaching one or more core mechanical engineering courses and contributing to the expansion of graduate-level offerings. Applicants with expertise in either experimental or computational research are encouraged to apply. Experience with the application of AI and machine-learning tools in research, and the incorporation of these technologies into teaching, will be considered a significant advantage. Applications will be reviewed as they are received; those received by December 1, 2026 , will be given full consideration. Applications should include the materials below: 1.           Cover letter 2.           Complete curriculum vitae 3.           Statement of research interests 4.           Statement of teaching philosophy 5.           Service statement (required only for tenured applicants) 6.           Contact information for at least three references"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193891": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Higher Education Policy",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: Peabody College of Education and Human Development: Leadership, Policy, and Organizations Location: Nashville. TN The Department of Leadership, Policy, and Organizations (LPO) at Peabody College of Education and Human Development at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant or Associate (with tenure) Professor specializing in higher education policy. This position will advance LPO’s multidisciplinary commitment to rigorous scholarship that is relevant to policy and practice given the changing landscape of higher education. The successful candidate will conduct rigorous quantitative research on pressing issues in higher education, including but not limited to policies and practices supporting student success in a broad range of postsecondary institutions, workforce preparation and transition, institutional governance, enrollment management, federal-institutional research partnership, or the application of artificial intelligence (AI) tools. We seek a scholar trained in higher education, public policy, or a social science discipline. Candidates should possess demonstrated capacity to produce rigorous research and publish in leading peer-reviewed journals. We are seeking applicants with a track record (commensurate with experience level) of external funding, interdisciplinary collaboration, and engagement with postsecondary institutions and policymakers. In addition to research, LPO operates programs and degrees at the undergraduate, master’s, and doctoral level, including a professional master’s degree program in Higher Education Administration. We expect the candidate to teach a combination of in-person and online courses across levels and to develop new courses to prepare students to understand, evaluate, and shape higher education policy and practice. Demonstrated experience or capacity for effective teaching and mentoring is required. Review of formal applications will begin October 31, 2026 . We expect the hire to start in August 2027. Applicants should submit the following materials through Interfolio via the link below: a letter addressing their research and teaching qualifications and interests, a full curriculum vitae, a writing sample, and the names and contact information of three references. References will only be contacted if the applicant is invited to an initial interview. For questions about the position, please contact the chair of the search committee: Brent Evans Associate Professor of Public Policy & Higher Education, Department of Leadership, Policy, and Organizations , b.evans@vanderbilt.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194058": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Clinical Psychology",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: Peabody College of Education and Human Development: Psychology and Human Development Location: Nashville, TN Tenure-Track Assistant Professor in Clinical Psychology Department of Psychology and Human Development Peabody College of Education and Human Development Vanderbilt University The Department of Psychology and Human Development in Peabody College at Vanderbilt University invites applications for two tenure-track positions as Assistant Professor in Clinical Psychology. The Clinical Psychology Ph.D. program at Vanderbilt spans two departments (the Department of Psychology and the Department of Psychology and Human Development), subscribes to the clinical science training model, and is PCSAS- and APA-accredited. We seek exceptional clinical scientists with demonstrated records of methodologically rigorous and conceptually strong research, clear potential to secure extramural funding, and a commitment to effective teaching and mentoring. We are particularly interested in clinical scientists who apply artificial intelligence (AI), computational, and/or advanced quantitative approaches to enhance the prediction, explanation, assessment, prevention, or treatment of psychopathology, particularly across development. Ideally, these scientists would foster collaborations with the College of Connected Computing . Applications could include using AI-related methods to enhance research methods, developing predictive models for risk and treatment response, and/or integrating AI into evidence-based interventions. Relevant approaches include those of AI’s subfields, such as machine learning, natural language processing, computer vision, speech and behavioral signal processing, digital phenotyping, passive sensing, advanced quantitative and computational modeling, personalized and technology-enabled interventions, among other innovative methods. Successful candidates will maintain active, externally funded research programs that apply innovative methods (e.g., quantitative, analytic, computational). We welcome applicants whose primary training is in clinical or developmental science with clinical applications. Responsibilities include leading an active, collaborative research program, teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels, research mentoring, and contributing to departmental, college, and university service. Vanderbilt University is a top-ranked private research university located near downtown Nashville, TN, a vibrant and rapidly growing city known for its music, food, culture, and access to outdoor recreation. Peabody College is consistently ranked among the top schools of education in the U.S., and the joint clinical psychology program is currently ranked #9 by U.S. News & World Report. The Department of Psychology and Human Development is home to highly collaborative faculty with strengths in developmental science, cognitive psychology, clinical psychological science, and quantitative methods. We maintain close connections with the Department of Psychology in the College of Arts and Science, the Department of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences at Vanderbilt University Medical Center, and the Vanderbilt Kennedy Center . Vanderbilt has outstanding research infrastructure and opportunities for collaboration, including the Vanderbilt University Institute of Imaging Science , Vanderbilt Brain Institute , Data Science Institute , LIVE Learning Innovation Incubator , and Advanced Computing Center for Research and Education . Minimum required qualifications include: • Ph.D. in Clinical Psychology, Developmental Psychology, or closely related area of psychology • Evidence of high-impact scholarship and a strong history of obtaining/or potential to obtain extramural funding • Demonstrated potential for effective teaching and mentoring at the undergraduate and graduate levels Applicants should submit the following materials via Interfolio: 1. Cover letter describing qualifications, research program, and fit with the department and focus of this search (1 to 2 pages) 2. Curriculum vitae 3. Research statement (2 to 3 pages) 4. Teaching and mentoring statement (1 to 2 pages) 5. Three samples of scholarly writing (published or unpublished manuscripts) 6. Contact information for three references (letters will be requested at a later stage) Review of applications will begin October 30, 2026. Full consideration will be given to applications received by that date. Inquiries about the search may be directed to the search committee chair Dr. Autumn Kujawa, Associate Professor of Psychology and Human Development, autumn.kujawa@vanderbilt.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/188578": {
      "title": "Visual Impairments Lecturer",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: Peabody College of Education and Human Development: Special Education Location: Special Education The Department of Special Education at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a one-year renewable appointment, for a 10-month lecturer position with a specific focus in the area of visual impairments. Contract term will run from August 1, 2027-May 31, 2028. The duties of the position are to teach courses in the visual impairments teacher preparation program; secure and supervise practicum placements; supervise students in practicum and student teaching; advise students in the visual impairments program, and serve on departmental and university committees. For more than 100 years, the Visual Impairments Program at Peabody College has been a leader in training and graduating professionals to work with students who are visual impaired, blind, and deafblind. As part of the Department of Special Education, we partner with school districts, local and state education agencies, disability organizations, community groups, and the Tennessee Department of Education to meet the needs of students with visual impairments, as well as to prepare the next generation of teachers and scholars. Courses in the Visual Impairments Program meet weekly and are primarily online (synchronously) 3 weeks per month. Students also come campus one Saturday per month for in-person classes. Due to the program’s low-residency model, candidates from outside the greater Nashville area are encouraged to apply with the expectation that an on-campus presence is only required for the limited days/year that students are on campus. Required: Teacher licensure in Visual Impairment Special Education. Experience working with students as a teacher of the visually impaired at the preK-12 level. An established record (or demonstrated potential) of activities such as presenting to practitioners and education leaders. Experience in developing instructional materials for adult learners. Experience in preparing or mentoring pre-service and/or in-service teachers of the visually impaired is necessary. Preferred: Doctorate-level professional, though candidates with a master’s degree and relevant experiences are strongly encouraged to apply. Teaching experience in higher education. Certification as a Certified Orientation and Mobility Specialist. Vanderbilt University shall abide by the requirements of 41 CFR §§ 60-1.4(a), 60-300.5(a) and 60-741.5(a). These regulations prohibit discrimination against qualified individuals based on their status as protected veterans or individuals with disabilities, and prohibit discrimination against all individuals based on their race, color, religion, sex, or national origin. Moreover, these regulations require that covered prime contractors and subcontractors take affirmative action to employ and advance in employment individuals without regard to race, color, religion, sex, national origin, protected veteran status or disability. Candidates should submit an application letter describing their qualifications, interest in teaching at the college level, and how their accomplishments address the qualifications noted above. They should forward a curriculum vitae, teaching evaluations or other evidence of teaching and mentoring effectiveness, and contact information for three people from whom letters of reference may be requested. Review of applications will begin December 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Inquiries regarding this position should be addressed to: Dr. Rachel Schles, Search Committee Chair, at rachel.schles@vanderbilt.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194053": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Faculty Position- Department of Molecular Physiology and Biophysics, Vanderbilt Center for Addiction Research (VCAR)",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: Basic Sciences: Molecular Physiology & Biophysics Location: Nashville, TN The Department of Molecular Physiology and Biophysics (MPB) and the Vanderbilt Center for Addiction Research (VCAR) invite applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position from outstanding early-career investigators whose research focuses on areas fundamental to understanding substance use disorders. We welcome applicants who are employing innovative experimental, computational, and translational approaches to investigate the molecular, cellular, circuit, systems, and behavioral mechanisms underlying substance use disorders and related biological processes. We seek candidates who are investigating fundamental mechanisms relevant to addiction, including—but not limited to—motivation and reward, reinforcement learning, decision-making, feeding and ingestive behavior, pain, stress and resilience, neural circuit function, synaptic plasticity, neuropharmacology, neuroimmune interactions, molecular signaling, and neuroendocrinology, and/or who are investigating topics relevant to addiction from the perspective of affective neuroscience, genetics and genomics, and/or computational and systems neuroscience. The successful candidate will have a primary appointment in the Department of Molecular Physiology and Biophysics (MPB) on the investigator track and will be a member of the Vanderbilt Center for Addiction Research (VCAR). VCAR is the intellectual hub for addiction research at Vanderbilt University: it brings together investigators across multiple schools and departments to elucidate the fundamental biology of substance use disorders. By fostering interdisciplinary collaborations among basic scientists, computational biologists, clinicians, and behavioral scientists, VCAR promotes innovative approaches to understanding the mechanisms of addiction, with the aim of developing new strategies for preventing and treating it. VCAR provides a dynamic scientific community with opportunities for collaboration, mentorship, trainee development, and shared research resources. MPB is an exceptionally collaborative and intellectually vibrant environment with internationally recognized strengths in fields ranging from molecular physiology, neuroscience, and metabolism to structural biology, quantitative biology, and biomedical engineering. The successful MPB/VCAR candidates will join a dynamic scientific community spanning the biological, biomedical, computational, engineering, and behavioral sciences. Vanderbilt offers state-of-the-art research facilities, outstanding shared resources, extensive opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration across the School of Medicine Basic Sciences and the broader University, and a longstanding commitment to mentoring early-career investigators. Applicants should demonstrate exceptional promise—more specifically, the capacity to establish an innovative, externally funded research program. The successful candidates will be expected to develop a rigorous independent research program, contribute to graduate and medical education, mentor trainees, and participate actively in the scientific community at Vanderbilt. Applications will begin to be reviewed immediately, and the process will continue until the position is filled. PhD or equivalent. Applicants should submit: A cover letter describing their interest in the position; Their curriculum vitae; A statement of research accomplishments and future research vision (2–4 pages); A statement describing their mentoring, teaching, and training philosophy; and Contact information for three to five references."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193899": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in the Department of Biochemistry, tenure-track faculty search 2026-2027",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: Basic Sciences: Biochemistry Location: Nashville, Tennessee The Department of Biochemistry at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor . Candidates are expected to establish a vigorous, externally funded research program and contribute to the education and mentorship of graduate students and postdoctoral trainees. The successful candidate should use innovative approaches to address biomedical problems at the molecular level. Current areas of departmental strength include RNA and DNA biology, signal transduction, protein folding and misfolding, structural biology, and chemical biology. We are especially interested in investigators whose work integrates cutting-edge concepts from synthetic biology, genomics, single molecule biochemistry, novel therapeutic modalities, and computational biology. The successful candidate will join a vibrant, growing, and collegial research community in the School of Medicine—Basic Sciences at Vanderbilt University. This is an ideal environment for discovery science and provides access to world-class core facilities . Trainees in the appointee’s lab will also be eligible for salary support through more than 25 externally funded graduate and postdoctoral training programs. Incoming faculty can expect a highly supportive peer group and extensive mentoring.  Vanderbilt is home to a welcoming and collaborative community of investigators committed to excellence in research, training, and service. Applicants should have a terminal degree (Ph.D., M.D., or equivalent), evidence of outstanding research discovery, and a compelling vision for their future research program. Evidence of success in obtaining research support is useful but not required. Experience in mentoring, teaching, and service is also valued. Please submit the following: 1. Curriculum vitae, including full list of publications 2. Research plan (2-3 pages, 11 pt font) 3. Mentoring philosophy (1 page) 4. PDFs of three peer-reviewed publications In addition, please a rrange for three confidential letters of recommendation to be submitted directly by the referees via Interfolio. Other optional documents, such as a cover letter, may be uploaded but are not required. Evaluation of applications will begin on November 1, 2026. We expect to determine in-person interviews by early December. ------------------------------------------------------------------- HOLD THE DATES : w e intend to conduct IN-PERSON faculty candidate visits on 2027 January 25-29 , in a symposium style where multiple candidates will present a seminar and conduct a more in-depth chalk talk to select faculty. HOLD THE DATES : w e intend to conduct VIRTUAL faculty candidate interviews via Zoom on 2026 November 30 - December 4 tbd (20 min casual \"elevator pitch\" style conversation with the search committee members)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/192934": {
      "title": "Full-Time Senior Lecturer in Neuroscience - Continuing Track",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Natural Sciences: Neuroscience Location: College of Arts and Science, Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN The Interdisciplinary Program in Neuroscience at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a full-time, non-tenure-track faculty position at the Senior Lecturer rank to contribute to undergraduate teaching, advising and mentoring in the Neuroscience program starting in the 2027 spring semester. The successful candidate will teach foundational courses in Vanderbilt’s research-based undergraduate BS degree major in Neuroscience. The Program is expanding its curriculum in Cellular and Molecular Neuroscience to include neuroimmunology and neurogenetics, as well as additional areas of interest in molecular neuroscience. The successful candidate will also contribute to the teaching of Introductory Neuroscience and play a key role in Neuroscience student major advising. This position will also support the Arts & Science Core curriculum, with the opportunity to teach in the first-year Core or develop an Exploratory Core course. With 300+ majors, the Interdisciplinary Program in Neuroscience is one of the largest majors in the College of Arts & Science and requires all students to complete laboratory research preparing students for careers in medicine and biomedical research. For questions regarding the position, please contact Douglas McMahon (douglas.g.mcmahon@vanderbilt.edu). Candidates should have a Ph.D. in Neuroscience or a related discipline (awarded no later than January 1, 2027). Please submit the following via Interfolio here: A cover letter with the names of three references (second-round candidates will be asked to submit three letters of recommendation via the Interfolio portal) A curriculum vitae A statement of teaching philosophy that includes ideas and goals for teaching an Introductory Neuroscience and a Cellular and Molecular neuroscience course (suggested length 2 pages) Teaching evaluations, if available Review of applications will begin immediately. Deadline for applications is November 1, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/179708": {
      "title": "Professor of Romance Languages, Cultures and Diasporas",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Humanities: Romance Languages, Cultures, and Diasporas Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN Vanderbilt University's College of Arts and Science seeks a distinguished scholar to serve as Professor and Chair of the newly formed Department of Romance Languages, Cultures, and Diasporas. The ideal candidate will lead a dynamic department encompassing French, Italian, Portuguese, and Spanish global languages, literatures, and cultural studies programs, and will have a proven record of working across disciplines.  The position will begin in the Fall 2026 semester. The College and the Department are committed to interdisciplinary excellence, and to current faculty interests in the intersections of literature and culture, encompassing art, race, gender, cinema, environment and migration.  The faculty is equally distinguished in research and teaching. The Department is housed in the College of Arts and Science, the largest school within a major research university.  The new department builds on a strong legacy of innovative scholarship and teaching.  This includes co-curricular engagement, faculty-led study abroad, and courses in languages for the professions.  The department also connects with other programs and institutional resources such as the Center for Languages; the Robert Penn Warren Center for the Humanities; the Center for Latin American, Caribbean, and Latinx Studies; the Max Kade Center for European and German Studies; and the W. T. Bandy Center for Baudelaire and Modern French Studies. Key Responsibilities: Provide visionary leadership for departmental governance, strategy, and collaboration Direct the innovative curriculum that bridges multiple Romance languages and disciplines and contribute to Vanderbilt’s core curriculum https://as.vanderbilt.edu/internal/undergraduates/academics/college-core.php Develop interdisciplinary initiatives connecting Romance language curricular offerings and extracurricular programming to broader college and university goals Foster place-based, immersive, experiential learning and study abroad opportunities within the department https://www.vanderbilt.edu/immersion/ Design retention and recruitment strategies for undergraduate students Support the professional growth of all faculty Oversee the department’s budget, support staff, and identify and pursue external funding opportunities Vanderbilt University is committed to fostering an academic environment that values fairness, interdisciplinary scholarship, and innovative pedagogy. S alary and Benefits are competitive, commensurate with qualifications and experience. Ph.D. in a Romance Language, Comparative Literature, or related field Demonstrated record of scholarly achievement meriting appointment as a tenured full professor Proven efficacy in administrative experience and academic program management Ability to develop strategic partnerships within and outside the university Comprehensive curriculum vitae A letter of application (2-3 pages) that includes a statement that speaks directly to your vision for this newly formed department, administrative experience, scholarly and teaching accomplishments, and ideas for promoting and expanding the study of literatures, languages, and cultures Please list the names and contact information of three references For full consideration, all materials must be submitted electronically by the application deadline.  Applications received by February 6, 2026 will receive full consideration. For questions contact the chair of the search committee at marshall.c.eakin@vanderbilt.edu"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191539": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of the Practice in Global Cinema and Media Histories",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Humanities: Cinema, Media Arts, and Theatre Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN The department of Cinema, Media Arts, and Theatre at Vanderbilt University seeks a scholar of global cinema and media histories at the rank of Assistant Professor of the Practice, non-tenure track, beginning August 2027. This position is a three-year appointment (with a 3-2 teaching load) with the possibility of renewal pending performance review and institutional need. We welcome approaches to the study and teaching of global cinema and media histories that encompass aesthetic, theoretical, and other humanistic concerns. The successful candidate should possess demonstrated expertise and strong teaching skills in humanistic approaches to global cinema and media histories. Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Film Studies, Media Studies, or a related field by August 1, 2027 and demonstrated excellence in teaching. Teaching load for this position is five courses annually. The teaching responsibilities would include a survey course in world cinema, an introduction to cinema and media arts course, and courses in the candidate’s area of specialization. Other responsibilities may include advising students, managing departmental and program extracurricular activities, contributing to programming, and other duties as assigned. This position will also support the Arts & Science Core curriculum. The screening of applicants will begin on October 20, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Application materials must include a letter of application; a curriculum vitae that includes contact information for three references; a teaching statement; evidence of teaching effectiveness in the form of course evaluations from 1-2 years of recent instruction (with raw data in addition to any student comments)."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/194830": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track Assistant Professor Specializing in the Study of Early Christianity",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Humanities: Classical and Religious Studies Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN The Department of Classical and Religious Studies at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor specializing in the study of early Christianity, beginning Fall 2027. We are interested in candidates with the ability to work across texts and traditions, including the traditions of Eastern Orthodoxy. We are open to candidates using innovative computational methods in the study of ancient texts. In addition to outstanding scholarly potential, successful candidates will demonstrate their ability to contribute to undergraduate teaching in religious studies—including undergraduate courses on Christianity, the New Testament, theories and methods in the study of religion, and other departmental curricular needs. The successful candidate will also contribute to the Arts & Science Core curriculum and the Graduate Department of Religion. Applicants should have Ph.D. in hand no later than August 1, 2027. Applications should be submitted through Interfolio [ https://apply.interfolio.com/194830] and include: a cover letter that discusses current and future research; CV; writing sample (25–30 pages); statement of teaching philosophy; evidence of teaching effectiveness (including recent teaching evaluations); and the names and contact details of three recommenders. Letters of recommendation will be solicited for short-listed candidates. Review of applications will begin November 15."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/174284": {
      "title": "Mellon Assistant Professor in Cinema and Media Arts",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Humanities: Cinema and Media Arts Location: Nashville, TN Posted: Sep 19, 2025 The department of Cinema & Media Arts at Vanderbilt University seeks a scholar of global media histories at the rank of Mellon Assistant Professor, non-tenure track, beginning August 2026 (3-year appointment, 2-2 load). We welcome a range of humanistic approaches to the study of film and media with a particular emphasis on scholars whose work addresses transnationality, decoloniality, migration, and/or diaspora. The successful candidate should possess an emerging scholarly profile in the humanistic study of global film and media histories. Applicants must have a Ph.D., completed no more than four years prior to the appointment date, in Film Studies, Media Studies, or a related field, by August 16, 2026; a scholarly record appropriate to an appointment at a research-intensive university; and demonstrated excellence in teaching. The screening of applicants will begin on October 20, 2025, and will continue until the position is filled. Application materials must include a letter of application, a curriculum vitae that includes contact information for three references, a statement on research, a teaching statement, and evidence of teaching effectiveness, including course evaluations if available."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/173703": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer position in Biological Sciences",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Natural Sciences: Biological Sciences Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN Posted: Sep 15, 2025 NON-TENURE TRACK SENIOR LECTURE FACULTY DEPARTMENT OF BIOLOGICAL SCIENCES, VANDERBILT UNIVERSITY The Department of Biological Sciences at Vanderbilt University invites applications to fill two non-tenure-track faculty positions in both Anatomy & Physiology/Cell Biology and Biochemistry/Chemical biology at the Senior Lecturer rank, to begin January 2, 2026. Candidates should have a Ph.D. in Biochemistry, Physiology, or related disciplines (awarded no later than December 15, 2025) and a strong commitment to undergraduate education. Successful candidates will design and teach lecture and laboratory courses in Anatomy & Physiology, Cell Biology, and/or Biochemistry as appropriate to the position and experience. Candidates with expertise in biostatistics will also be given strong consideration.  New faculty will provide additional instructional support for the department as needed ( https://as.vanderbilt.edu/biological-sciences/ ). The Department of Biological Sciences, housed within the College of Arts and Science, is a community of tenure track and lecture track faculty with a strong commitment to excellence in teaching and research at all levels of biological organization. The successful applicant will have an opportunity to teach and mentor a growing number of undergraduate students with interests in health-related fields and environmental sciences. Strong collaborative and cross-disciplinary interactions are enhanced by the close physical proximity to the School of Medicine, located steps away from the Department of Biological Sciences. Vanderbilt University is a private research university ranked in the top 20 nationally; its 330-acre park-like campus is located less than two miles of downtown Nashville, TN, a thriving city known for its rich music history and culture. Applicants should submit: (a) a curriculum vitae, (b) a statement of teaching philosophy that includes plans/ideas/goals for designing and developing an anatomy & physiology lab course or strategies for teaching Biochemistry and Biostatistics, (c) teaching evaluations, if available, and (d) a statement addressing how you have used evidence-based teaching practices to maintain effective pedagogy. Please include a cover letter with the names of three references; second-round candidates will be asked to submit three letters of recommendation via the Interfolio portal. Screening of applications will begin immediately."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/175161": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer position in Economics",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Social Sciences: Economics Location: Nashville, TN Posted: Oct 15, 2025 The Department of Economics at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a full-time, non-tenure-track Senior Lecturer position to begin August 16, 2026.  A successful candidate will have a PhD. in Economics awarded no later than August 1, 2026, and a strong desire to teach undergraduate courses in core areas of economics as well as the ability to offer elective courses in their fields of interest. The standard course load will be six courses per year and would likely include multiple sections of some courses. The initial term of appointment will be for one to three years with the possibility of renewal contingent upon performance review and curricular need.  Compensation will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Vanderbilt stresses excellence in teaching for non-tenure-track faculty in its undergraduate programs. The position will remain open until filled. To apply, please submit CV, sample research paper, statement of teaching interests, evidence of teaching effectiveness including student course evaluatons, if available, and three confidential letters of recommendation. Interested applicants should submit their applications via Interfolio at this link https://apply.interfolio.com/175161 . We will commence reviewing applications on a rolling basis starting January 1, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/174419": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer in U.S. Economic History",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Humanities: History Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN Posted: Sep 22, 2025 The department of History at Vanderbilt University seeks candidates for an instructional position at the rank of Senior Lecturer in U.S. Economic History. We seek a U.S. historian with a demonstrated record of teaching excellence who specializes in economic history, of any period. We are especially interested in candidates comfortable teaching a diversity of methods, both quantitative and qualitative. The position will begin in Fall 2026 and is on the continuing track (non-tenure-track). The teaching load is six courses per academic year, including an introduction to economic history, methods in economic history, a majors' capstone on an economic theme, and elective courses in the candidate's area of expertise, such as U.S. business and finance, capitalism, entrepreneurship, innovation, labor and management, poverty, social inequality, neoliberalism, protectionism, and globalization. The successful candidate will make key contributions to \"Economics and History,\" a thriving interdisciplinary major at Vanderbilt. Students in the program are encouraged to study topics that stretch across national and chronological boundaries, and to think comparatively. The initial appointment is for three years, with the potential for continuing reappointment, contingent upon institutional review. All faculty in the Department of History teach classes at all levels of curriculum, from introductory lectures to upper-level seminars, and also serve in an advisory capacity for honors students and undergraduate majors. All faculty also participate in the A&S Core, the general education program of the College of Arts and Science. Candidates should have evidence of excellent teaching experience, and a commitment to original research in U.S. and economic history. All requirements for the PhD in history must be completed no later than August 1, 2026. Applications are accepted via Interfolio. To be considered, please submit the following materials: Cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching statement, teaching evaluations for all available courses, two sample syllabi (at least one for an introductory lecture course), and three letters of reference. Review of application files will begin on December 1, 2025."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/174682": {
      "title": "Associate Professor for the Practice of Business Studies, Finance",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Social Sciences: Business Studies Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN Posted: Sep 26, 2025 The undergraduate Business Studies Program at Vanderbilt University invites applications for the position of Associate Professor of the Practice in Finance, starting in the 2026 fall semester.  This is a full-time, non-tenure-track position with a renewable three-year appointment, contingent upon performance and curricular need. The successful candidate will have research interest and teaching experience in finance and related topics. For more information on our programs, visit our website . Responsibilities: Teach a full course load (3-3) of undergraduate finance courses, which may include core finance principles, corporate finance, financial management, valuation, and investments. Develop and deliver engaging, interactive, and student-centered lessons that encourage critical thinking and practical application of finance concepts. Stay updated on the latest trends, technologies, and best practices in undergraduate finance pedagogy, incorporating new techniques into course materials. Participate in curriculum development and review to ensure alignment with industry needs. Provide academic advising and mentorship to undergraduate students. Contribute to the program’s intellectual life by serving on committees, participating in departmental events, leading program initiatives, and collaborating with colleagues. Maintain a regular on-campus presence for in-person teaching, office hours, and other teaching responsibilities. Ph.D. or MBA in a related field is required. 5+ years of undergraduate finance teaching experience 10+ years of relevant industry experience in finance. Proven excellence in course design, delivery, and engagement with students. Strong skills in all aspects of finance, financial strategies and tools. Excellent communication, interpersonal, and collaboration abilities. A passion for undergraduate education and a commitment to student success. To apply, please submit the following: Cover letter outlining your qualifications and interest. Your curriculum vitae. Your teaching philosophy statement. Contact information for six professional references Course evaluations, including raw data Review of applications will begin on October 13, 2025, and will continue until the position is filled. The start date for this position is August 16, 2026."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/175447": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer of Medicine, Health, and Society - Health & Social Media",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Arts and Science: Social Sciences: Medicine, Health and Society Location: Vanderbilt University, Nashville, TN Posted: Oct 08, 2025 The Department of Medicine, Health, and Society (MHS) at Vanderbilt University invites applications for the position of Senior Lecturer to develop and teach critical undergraduate and graduate courses focused on Health and Social Media, Healthism, and/or the effects of Health Influencers.  This is a full-time position, not on the tenure track, for a three-year appointment beginning Fall 2026 with the possibility for renewal contingent upon performance review and curricular need. The position carries a 3-3 annual teaching load. MHS is an interdisciplinary department that draws from the social sciences, humanities, and health sciences to explore the structural, cultural, political, and ethical dimensions of health and illness. With the proliferation of health technologies, digital wellness economies, and AI-driven diagnostics and care platforms, this position fills a vital curricular and intellectual need. It aligns closely with the department’s commitment to addressing timely public concerns and preparing students for leadership in medicine, public health, data ethics, and health tech. To that end, we seek an innovative and engaged educator whose work centers on one or more of the following interrelated domains: Health and Social Media: Including digital health narratives, health information, virtual wellness communities, platform politics, and algorithmic influence on health decisions and behaviors Healthism and Wellness Culture: Including health influencers, critiques of the moralization of health, neoliberal wellness discourses, and the commodification of self-care Health and Artificial Intelligence (AI): Including algorithmic bias in healthcare delivery, predictive analytics, surveillance medicine, data ethics, and the sociopolitical consequences of machine learning in clinical and everyday settings We especially welcome candidates who approach these issues through frameworks such as critical public health, media studies, tech, law, medical humanities, medical sociology, health economics, and/or bioethics. Teaching Responsibilities The successful candidate will teach a 3:3 course load and contribute to: Core undergraduate courses such as Health and Society, Health and Social Justice, or Medicine, Technology, and Society New electives including but not limited to: Health and Social Media The Politics of Wellness AI and the Future of Care Digital Bodies and Data Surveillance, Medicine, and Power Undergraduate thesis and capstone advising Instruction in the A&S College Core curriculum We value innovative teaching approaches that include project-based learning, digital pedagogy, and engagement with community-based or public-facing initiatives.  This position also directly supports the department’s goal of preparing students to engage critically and ethically with the technologies shaping the future of health. More information about MHS can be found via our website, http://www.vanderbilt.edu/mhs , and about the MA programs at https://www.vanderbilt.edu/mhs/graduate/m-a-program . PhD, in hand by August 1, 2026, in a relevant discipline (e.g., sociology, anthropology, public health, media studies, STS, medical humanities, data ethics, economics) Demonstrated excellence in interdisciplinary teaching at the undergraduate level Applicants should electronically submit via Interfolio the following]: A letter of interest addressed to Jonathan Metzl, Chair of MHS A curriculum vitae Evidence of teaching effectiveness, including all course evaluations, if available The names of three references Applications will be reviewed starting November 24, 2025 and continue until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196117": {
      "title": "Tenure-track Assistant Professor or Tenured Associate Professor Position in Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering at Vanderbilt University",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: School of Engineering: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering Location: Nashville, TN Posted: Oct 22, 2026 Assistant or Associate Professor Position in Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering at Vanderbilt University The Department of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering at Vanderbilt University invites applications for a faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor (tenure-track) or Associate Professor (tenured). We seek outstanding candidates whose research advances the frontiers of chemical and biomolecular engineering, particularly in engineering biology and biomanufacturing; computational and AI-enabled chemical and biomolecular engineering; and process systems engineering for advanced manufacturing and resource recovery. We especially welcome research that addresses space exploration and national defense challenges in austere environments—where water, oxygen, energy, materials, and infrastructure are scarce or difficult to access—while leveraging Vanderbilt's growing strengths in computing, engineering, medicine, sustainability, and national security. The Department of Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering (ChBE) at Vanderbilt is a highly collaborative and growing department with strengths spanning biotechnology and biomolecular engineering, computational science and engineering, materials and nanotechnology, and energy and sustainability. The department includes 14 tenured/tenure-track faculty who conduct more than $11 million in sponsored research annually and contribute to thriving undergraduate and graduate programs. ChBE is embedded in Vanderbilt's highly collaborative research environment, with immediate access to the Vanderbilt Institute for Nanoscale Science and Engineering (VINSE) and its state-of-the-art core facilities. Faculty also engage with interdisciplinary initiatives including the Vanderbilt Center for Sustainability, Energy and Climate; the Vanderbilt Lab for Immersive AI Translation; and the Vanderbilt Institute for National Security. Vanderbilt University Medical Center, co-located on campus, provides exceptional opportunities for translational collaborations spanning engineering, biology, and medicine. The newly established College of Connected Computing—Vanderbilt's first new college in 40 years—creates particularly strong opportunities for interdisciplinary research and education at the interface of chemical engineering, computing, artificial intelligence, and data science. Vanderbilt University is a top-20 national research university located on a 340-acre campus 1.5 miles from downtown Nashville. Long known as a global center for healthcare and music, Nashville has experienced substantial growth in technology, advanced manufacturing, electric vehicles, and related industries, expanding opportunities for university-industry partnerships and dual-career households. Applicants should hold a Ph.D. degree in chemical engineering or a closely related engineering or scientific discipline and should have research and teaching goals strongly aligned with the fields of chemical and biomolecular engineering. Candidates should demonstrate a record of scholarly accomplishment appropriate to career stage and the potential to establish or sustain an internationally recognized, externally funded research program. Successful candidates will also demonstrate a commitment to excellence in undergraduate and graduate education and to collaborative engagement across Vanderbilt. The position will be at the Assistant or Associate Professor level depending on the experience and record of the candidate. Application materials are to be submitted to apply.interfolio.com/196117 and must include: ·         Cover Letter ·         Complete Curriculum Vitae ·         4- to 6-page statement of research interests detailing past contributions, future research plans, potential funding directions, and Vanderbilt-specific collaboration opportunities ·         2- to 3-page statement of teaching interests and philosophy highlighting synergies within the ChBE department and interests in developing new courses ·         Names and contact information of at least three professional references Review of applications will begin November 1, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. For full consideration, applications should be received by December 1, 2026. Please address questions to Professor Jamey Young, Chair of ChBE, by email to j.d.young@vanderbilt.edu ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195646": {
      "title": "Tenure-Line (Open Rank)",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: Peabody College of Education and Human Development: Human and Organizational Development Location: Nashville, Tennessee Posted: Oct 27, 2026 The Department of Human and Organizational Development (HOD) at Vanderbilt University invites applications for an open-rank, tenure-line or tenured professor position at the assistant, associate, or full professor level in Human and Organizational Development. Human and Organizational Development is an interdisciplinary department that focuses on exploring human and organizational development in context (e.g., school, neighborhood, civic organizations, community, and social policy). Our faculty holds expertise in community psychology, sociology, geography, anthropology, communication studies, and public health. We welcome applications from social scientists with active programs of research that can contribute to inter- and trans-disciplinary inquiry and educational programs. We are especially interested in scholars conducting community-engaged research using quantitative and mixed-methods that address complex social problems across multiple scales or levels. Candidates should have a strong research program appropriate for their career stage, demonstrated by publications and a track record of, or strong potential for, securing external research support. Successful candidates will also have excellent teaching credentials and the demonstrated capacity to provide leadership and mentoring to graduate and undergraduate students. The successful candidate will teach and mentor students in Vanderbilt’s undergraduate program in Human and Organizational Development , the doctoral program in Community Research and Action , and, depending on area of research, one or more of our master’s programs in Community Development and Action , Organizational Development for Social Innovation , and/or Human Development Counseling . This faculty position offers the opportunity to join a highly productive, interdisciplinary faculty at a research-intensive university. The application review process will begin December 15, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. (Applications submitted by December 15, 2026 will receive full consideration). Please submit application materials, including cover letter, curriculum vitae, research statement, teaching statement, select publications, and the names of three people who can provide a reference letter to the following link: https://apply.interfolio.com/195646 . Additional questions may be directed to (donna.f.smith@vanderbilt.edu), 615-322-2677 or information about the department may be found at https://peabody.vanderbilt.edu/academics/departments/human-organizational-development/."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196041": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of the Practice - The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: School of Engineering: Electrical and Computer Engineering Location: Nashville, TN  Posted: Oct 20, 2026 THE DEPARTMENT OF ELECTRICAL AND COMPUTER ENGINEERING (ECE) AT VANDERBILT UNIVERSITY is seeking applicants for an Assistant Professor of the Practice of Electrical and Computer Engineering position with target start dates of Spring and Fall Semesters of 2027. Primary responsibilities are to develop and teach classes in the ECE undergraduate curriculum and to assist with programmatic certification. This is a term appointment, eligible for renewal, contingent on performance. We are committed to teaching and research excellence and have fully integrated professors of the practice and lecturers into ECE faculty operations. Ranked #16 nationally, Vanderbilt University is a private, internationally recognized research university located on 330 park-like acres 1.5 miles from downtown Nashville, Tennessee. Its 10 distinct schools share a single cohesive campus that values collaboration. The university enrolls over 13,800 undergraduate, graduate, and professional students, including 43% minority students and over 1,700 international students from 113 countries. With a metro population of over 2.2 million people, Nashville’s top industries by employment include trade, transportation, and utilities; education and health services; professional and business services; government; and leisure and hospitality. Other industries include manufacturing, financial activities, construction, and information. Long known as a hub for health care and music, Nashville is a technology center with a considerable pool of health care, AI, and defense-related jobs available. In recent years, the city has experienced an influx of major office openings by some of the largest global tech companies and prime Silicon Valley startups. Vanderbilt University has made the health and safety of our students, faculty and staff and our surrounding communities a top priority. As part of that commitment, the University may require employees to comply with any testing or health safety measures which the University may require. Ideal qualifications include a PH.D. degree in computer engineering, electrical engineering, electrical and computer engineering, or a related discipline. Prior teaching experience in these programs would be a plus. Applications should include a full CV, statement of teaching experience and interests, a list of three names and email address for reference, and a personal statement.  Review of applications will begin immediately, and applications will be accepted until the position is filled."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196229": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in the College of Connected Computing",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Connected Computing Location: Nashville, TN Posted: Oct 24, 2026 This is the page for Assistant Professor (tenure-track) applications. If you wish to apply for the Associate or Full Professor positions, please go here: https://apply.interfolio.com/196244 . Vanderbilt University invites applications for multiple tenure-track Assistant Professor positions in computing and related disciplines. New faculty will join Vanderbilt’s new College of Connected Computing, founded on a “computing for all” vision that brings computer science, data science, and artificial intelligence together with a growing set of interdisciplinary areas, such as computational biology, human-computer interaction, computing + society, and computational mind and behavior. Learn more about the College and its research at computing.vanderbilt.edu. Successful applicants are expected to develop vigorous, externally funded research programs, teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels, and mentor doctoral students. The search will place particular emphasis on candidates’ potential to secure external research funding from federal agencies, foundations, industry, or other sources. Every assistant professor joins an area with tenured colleagues, receives named faculty mentors, and is supported by a start-up package that includes guaranteed doctoral student funding. This is an open search in all areas of computing research, with particular interest in the following six broad areas: 1. Physical Intelligence. Robotics, embodied and physical AI, computer vision, robot learning, and autonomous systems, including autonomy in cities and other complex real-world environments. 2. Foundations of Computing. Theoretical computer science, quantum algorithms and quantum information, optimization, statistics and probabilistic machine learning, numerical and scientific machine learning, and formal methods, with opportunities for courtesy appointments in Mathematics. 3. Humans, Computing & Society. Computational mind and behavior, including the computational principles underlying perception, cognition, emotion, language, and social interaction at the brain and behavioral levels; human-computer interaction, including human-centered AI, interactive technologies for civic life, learning, work, health, and accessibility; and computing + society, including how computing and society shape one another across culture, organizations, policy, and infrastructure. 4. Secure & Trustworthy Systems. Security across systems, networks, and software; applied cryptography; usable security and privacy; security of cyber-physical systems and urban infrastructure; and verification of critical and learning-enabled systems. 5. AI in Education and Computational Learning Technologies. Artificial intelligence in education; learning analytics and educational data mining; human-centered design of computational learning technologies; adaptive and personalized learning technologies; technologies for self-regulated and collaborative learning; AI and computer science education; AI-based educational measurement and assessment; and AI-enabled training and upskilling, with the possibility of joint appointments with Peabody College. 6. Computational Biology. Genomics and bioinformatics algorithms, systems biology, biomedical informatics and natural language processing, AI for health, and other computational approaches to biological and biomedical data. New faculty join a growing group of senior computational biologists in the College. We welcome applications across computing, including work that spans or extends beyond the areas listed above. To facilitate review, identify one of the six areas as your primary review area in your cover letter, choosing the closest fit. You may also identify a secondary area and briefly explain the connections. Submit only one application to the appropriate rank-specific search; the College will coordinate review across areas as appropriate. Vanderbilt University is a private research university located on 330 park-like acres on the western edge of downtown Nashville, Tennessee. Nashville is a regional center for culture and technology that has grown alongside the university since Vanderbilt’s founding in 1873. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in computing or a related field, including computer science, data science, artificial intelligence, human-computer interaction, information science, engineering, mathematics, statistics, and the behavioral, social, learning, and life sciences, by the start date. Candidates must demonstrate a record of peer-reviewed scholarship and the potential to develop an independent, influential research program in computing or at the intersection of computing and another discipline. We welcome interest in interdisciplinary collaboration. Apply through Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/196229 . Submit the following materials: • Cover letter (one page). Identify your primary review area and any secondary area, explain your interest in Vanderbilt, and describe how your proposed research and teaching would contribute to the College. • Curriculum vitae. • Research statement (maximum five pages, excluding references). Concisely describe your most significant research contributions and future research agenda, including plans for external research support. Shorter statements are welcome. • Teaching statement (maximum two pages). Describe your approach to teaching and mentoring, relevant experience, and future plans. • Service statement (maximum two pages). The service statement should describe any service experience and your interests in activities such as professional service, outreach, or support for students and colleagues. • Three representative publications or preprints. • Teaching evaluations, if available. • Three to five references. Submit names, affiliations, and email addresses for three to five references. **References will be contacted automatically, via Interfolio, to request confidential letters of recommendation upon submission of your application.** Review of applications will begin immediately. Complete applications, including reference contact details, received by December 1, 2026 will receive full consideration; review will continue on a rolling basis until the positions are filled. The anticipated start date is August 2027, although later start dates can be arranged. For inquiries, including questions about the appropriate rank-specific search, please contact cccfacultyaffairs@vanderbilt.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196244": {
      "title": "Associate Professor or Full Professor in the College of Connected Computing",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Connected Computing Location: Nashville, TN Posted: Oct 24, 2026 This is the page for Associate and Full Professor (tenured) applications. If you wish to apply for the Assistant Professor (tenure-track) positions, please go here: https://apply.interfolio.com/196229 . Vanderbilt University invites applications for multiple tenured Associate or Full Professor positions in computing and related disciplines. New faculty will join Vanderbilt’s new College of Connected Computing, founded on a “computing for all” vision that brings computer science, data science, and artificial intelligence together with a growing set of interdisciplinary areas, such as computational biology, human-computer interaction, computing + society, and computational mind and behavior. Learn more about the College and its research at computing.vanderbilt.edu. Senior faculty will have the opportunity to shape the College’s research agenda, build collaborative research programs, and develop its undergraduate and graduate programs. They are expected to teach at the undergraduate and graduate levels, mentor junior faculty and doctoral students, and contribute leadership appropriate to their rank and expertise. This is an open search in all areas of computing research, with particular interest in the following six broad areas: 1. Physical Intelligence. Robotics, embodied and physical AI, computer vision, robot learning, and autonomous systems, including autonomy in cities and other complex real-world environments. Senior faculty will help anchor a new shared robotics facility. 2. Foundations of Computing. Theoretical computer science, quantum algorithms and quantum information, optimization, statistics and probabilistic machine learning, numerical and scientific machine learning, and formal methods, with opportunities for courtesy appointments in Mathematics. We particularly seek a senior scholar in quantum computing to anchor the area and connect it to Vanderbilt’s planned quantum initiatives. 3. Humans, Computing & Society. Computational mind and behavior, including the computational principles underlying perception, cognition, emotion, language, and social interaction at the brain and behavioral levels; human-computer interaction, including human-centered AI, interactive technologies for civic life, learning, work, health, and accessibility; and computing + society, including how computing and society shape one another across culture, organizations, policy, and infrastructure. 4. Secure & Trustworthy Systems. Security across systems, networks, and software; applied cryptography; usable security and privacy; security of cyber-physical systems and urban infrastructure; and verification of critical and learning-enabled systems. 5. AI in Education and Computational Learning Technologies. Artificial intelligence in education; learning analytics and educational data mining; human-centered design of computational learning technologies; adaptive and personalized learning technologies; technologies for self-regulated and collaborative learning; AI and computer science education; AI-based educational measurement and assessment; and AI-enabled training and upskilling, with the possibility of joint appointments with Peabody College. We seek a senior scholar to lead and build this new area. 6. Computational Biology. Genomics and bioinformatics algorithms, systems biology, biomedical informatics and natural language processing, AI for health, and other computational approaches to biological and biomedical data. We welcome applications across computing, including work that spans or extends beyond the areas listed above. To facilitate review, identify one of the six areas as your primary review area in your cover letter, choosing the closest fit. You may also identify a secondary area and briefly explain the connections. Submit only one application to the appropriate rank-specific search; the College will coordinate review across areas as appropriate. Vanderbilt University is a private research university located on 330 park-like acres on the western edge of downtown Nashville, Tennessee. Nashville is a regional center for culture and technology that has grown alongside the university since Vanderbilt’s founding in 1873. Candidates must hold a Ph.D. in computing or a related field, including computer science, data science, artificial intelligence, human-computer interaction, information science, engineering, mathematics, statistics, and the behavioral, social, learning, and life sciences. Candidates must have a record of achievement commensurate with appointment as an Associate or Full Professor with tenure at Vanderbilt. We welcome applicants from universities, industry, and national laboratories. Candidates must have a strong record of peer-reviewed publications and should demonstrate collaborative research and mentoring accomplishments appropriate to their rank and professional setting. A strong, sustained record of securing external research funding will be a major consideration in this search. We welcome candidates supported by federal agencies, foundations, industry, or other sources as we build a robust funding portfolio across the College. Apply through Interfolio at https://apply.interfolio.com/196244 . Submit the following materials: • Cover letter (one page). Identify your primary review area and any secondary area, explain your interest in Vanderbilt, and describe the research, collaborations, or academic programs you would help develop. • Curriculum vitae. • Research statement (maximum five pages, excluding references). Concisely describe your most significant research contributions and future research agenda, including plans for external research support. Shorter statements are welcome. • Teaching statement (maximum two pages). Describe your approach to teaching and mentoring, relevant experience, and future plans. • Service statement (maximum two pages). The service statement should describe your service and leadership contributions to your institution, profession, or broader community, such as committee work, editorial or conference service, professional society activities, or outreach. • Teaching evaluations, if available. • Contact details for three to five references. Provide names, affiliations, and email addresses. Submit reference contact details with your application; reference letters are not required at the time of submission. References will be contacted only with your permission. Review of applications will begin immediately. Complete applications, including reference contact details, received by December 1, 2026 will receive full consideration; review will continue on a rolling basis until the positions are filled. The anticipated start date is August 2027, although later start dates can be arranged. For inquiries, including questions about the appropriate rank-specific search, please contact cccfacultyaffairs@vanderbilt.edu."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196445": {
      "title": "Professor of the Practice (All Ranks/Two Positions) in the College of Connected Computing",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Connected Computing Location: Nashville, TN Posted: Oct 27, 2026 Join the inaugural faculty cohort at Vanderbilt University’s new College of Connected Computing. Become part of the community designed to foster radical collaboration at the intersection of interdisciplinary studies involving computing , AI, data science, and related fields.  We are seeking well-qualified applicants at all ranks to join our growing cohort of teaching, tenure and tenure track faculty in building and supporting this vision. We have opened two full-time Professor of the Practice faculty positions. As a member of our growing college, teaching faculty will enjoy a 2/3 teaching load. Our average class size is 45 students with over 20 courses having 35 or fewer students.  Faculty are well supported by TAs and undergraduate graders.  Teaching faculty typically teach undergraduate core courses in data science, generative AI, and introductory programming in Python, R or Java. They may also choose to teach in one of our master’s programs in Data Science or Applied AI.  Professors of the Practice have opportunity to teach upper-level courses and to develop additional courses as our curriculum evolves under the new vision for the College of Connected Computing. Our teaching faculty can supplement their 9-month salary with additional teaching opportunities, many of which are offered online. Teaching faculty will participate in service to the school, engaging in novel efforts to broaden participation, deepen undergraduate research opportunities and widen access to computational thinking studies for students across the university. We are building innovative programs and launching fresh initiatives that will appeal to the interests of a diverse faculty. This is your opportunity to build or reshape your career and participate in our vision of computing for all. Other benefits of the position include: · Yearly travel funds to support professional development and research activities · Competitive salaries based on the CRA Taulbee survey · 3-5 year contracts with opportunities for renewal at least 1.5 years before the previous contract expires · Full participation in faculty governance and decision making · A comprehensive mentoring program · A university fringe benefits package that includes outstanding healthcare, prescription drug coverage, dental, vision, HSA, Dependent care FSA and tuition benefits for you, your spouse and children · Your choice of lifestyle from downtown Nashville condos around the corner to quiet, rural farm life just a 30-minute drive away The ideal candidate will hold a Ph.D. in Data Science, Social Science, Digital Humanities, or closely related fields.  All qualified candidates will be considered. However, we are especially interested in applicants with backgrounds in Generative and Applied AI. Applicants who will receive their degree by August 1, 2027, are also encouraged to apply. Applications should be submitted at apply.interfolio.com/196445 . Applications should include a full CV, statement of teaching experience or philosophy and interests, as well as the names and email addresses of three references. Review of applications will begin immediately. Applications received by 30 November 2026, will be assured of full consideration. Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. Applicants who consider themselves more closely aligned with computer science or closely related fields are encouraged to explore our separate openings for Professor of the Practice in the Computer Science program within the College of Connected Computing at Vanderbilt apply.interfolio.com/196447 ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196447": {
      "title": "Professor of the Practice (All Ranks) in Computer Science",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: College of Connected Computing Location: Nashville, TN Posted: Oct 27, 2026 Join the inaugural faculty cohort at Vanderbilt University’s new College of Connected Computing. Become part of the community designed to foster radical collaboration at the intersection of interdisciplinary studies involving computing , AI, data science, and related fields.  We are seeking well-qualified applicants at all ranks to join our growing cohort of teaching, tenure and tenure track faculty in building and supporting this vision. We have opened one full-time Professor of the Practice faculty position in computer science. As a member of our growing college, teaching faculty will enjoy a 2/3 teaching load. Our average class size is 45 students with over 20 courses having 35 or fewer students.  Faculty are well supported by TAs and undergraduate graders. Teaching faculty typically teach undergraduate core courses in computer science, with the opportunity to teach upper-level courses and to develop additional courses as our curriculum evolves under the new vision for the College of Connected Computing. Our teaching faculty can supplement their 9-month salary with additional teaching opportunities, many of which are offered online. Teaching faculty will participate in service to the college, engaging in novel efforts to broaden participation, deepen undergraduate research opportunities and widen access to computational thinking studies for students across the university. We are building innovative programs and launching fresh initiatives that will appeal to the interests of a diverse faculty. This is your opportunity to build or reshape your career and participate in our vision of computing for all. Other benefits of the position include: · Yearly travel funds to support professional development and research activities · Competitive salaries based on the CRA Taulbee survey · 3-5 year contracts with opportunities for renewal at least 1.5 years before the previous contract expires · Full participation in faculty governance and decision making · A comprehensive mentoring program · A university fringe benefits package that includes outstanding healthcare, prescription drug coverage, dental, vision, HSA, Dependent care FSA and tuition benefits for you, your spouse and children · Your choice of lifestyle from downtown Nashville condos around the corner to quiet, rural farm life just a 30-minute drive away The ideal candidate will hold a Ph.D. in Computer Science or closely related fields.  All qualified candidates will be considered. Applicants who will receive their degree by August 1, 2027, are also encouraged to apply. Applications should be submitted at apply.interfolio.com/196447 . Applications should include a full CV, statement of teaching experience and interests, as well as the names and email addresses of three references. Review of applications will begin immediately. Applications received by 30 November 2026, will be assured of full consideration. Review of applications will continue until the positions are filled. Applicants who consider themselves more closely aligned with data science, digital humanities, applied AI or closely related fields are encouraged to explore our separate openings for Professor of the Practice in the Data Science and applied AI programs within the College of Connected Computing at Vanderbilt apply.interfolio.com/196445."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196498": {
      "title": "Open-Rank Faculty Position - Cyber-Physical Avionic Systems",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: School of Engineering: Electrical and Computer Engineering Location: Nashville, TN  Posted: Oct 27, 2026 The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at Vanderbilt University School of Engineering invites applications for an open-ranked, faculty position in Cyber-Physical Avionic Systems and Space Application.  This initiative is part of our commitment to establishing a school-wide, interdisciplinary Defense Tech Cluster aimed at advancing technologies for national defense and security that can be scaled and translated into products. The successful candidate(s) will have expertise that aligns with our strategic mission to enhance research and education in Electrical and Computer Engineering. The areas of particular focus include i ntegrated cyber-physical spacecraft systems for the New Space industry, including RF systems-on-chip, software-defined radios, payload processors, edge AI, high-rate sensors, distributed flight computers, and integrated autonomy. The successful candidates will be expected to build research programs that interface with one or several institutes strongly connected with the department and often led by ECE faculty members. These include the recently-formed Vanderbilt Institute for Innovations Beyond Earth ( VIIBE ), the Institute for Space and Defense Electronics ( ISDE ), the Vanderbilt Institute for Nanoscale Science and Engineering ( VINSE ), the Vanderbilt Lab for Immersive AI Translation ( VALIANT ), the Institute for Convergent Software Integrated Systems ( ICONS , formerly known as the Institute for Software Integrated Systems), and the Vanderbilt Institute of National Security ( INS ). Ranked #16 nationally, Vanderbilt University is a private, internationally recognized research university located on 330 park-like acres 1.5 miles from downtown Nashville, Tennessee. Its 10 distinct schools share a single cohesive campus that values collaboration. The university enrolls over 13,800 undergraduate, graduate, and professional students, including 43% minority students and over 1,700 international students from 113 countries. With a metro population of over 2.2 million people, Nashville’s top industries by employment include trade, transportation, and utilities; education and health services; professional and business services; government; and leisure and hospitality. Other industries include manufacturing, financial activities, construction, and information. Long known as a hub for health care and music, Nashville is a technology center with a considerable pool of health care, AI, and defense-related jobs available. In recent years, the city has experienced an influx of major office openings by some of the largest global tech companies and prime Silicon Valley startups. Candidates must have a PhD (or equivalent) in Electrical and Computer Engineering or related field by the time of appointment. The successful candidate will be expected to: · Establish a nationally recognized research program supported by extramural funding. · Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in various ECE courses. · Contribute to departmental, school-wide, and university-wide initiatives, fostering a collegial and collaborative environment. Applications should include the following materials: 1. Cover letter 2. Complete curriculum vitae 3. Statement of research interests, highlighting potential collaborative opportunities within and beyond the Defense Tech Cluster 4. Statement of teaching philosophy, including innovative approaches to teaching and curriculum development 5. Service statement 6. Contact information for at least three references"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196499": {
      "title": "Open-Rank Faculty Position- Integrated-Circuit Design & On-Chip Communications",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: School of Engineering: Electrical and Computer Engineering Location: Nashville, TN  Posted: Oct 27, 2026 The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at Vanderbilt University School of Engineering invites applications for an open-ranked, faculty position in Integrated-Circuit Design & On-Chip Communications.  This initiative is part of our commitment to establishing a school-wide, interdisciplinary Defense Tech Cluster aimed at advancing technologies for national defense and security that can be scaled and translated into products. The successful candidate(s) will have expertise that aligns with our strategic mission to enhance research and education in Electrical and Computer Engineering. The areas of particular focus include d esign, synthesis, and evaluation of integrated circuits and IP blocks, with strong connections to AI hardware, data centers, defense hardware, and space-based systems. The successful candidates also will be expected to build research programs that interface with one or several institutes strongly connected with the department and often led by ECE faculty members. These include the recently-formed Vanderbilt Institute for Innovations Beyond Earth ( VIIBE ), the Institute for Space and Defense Electronics ( ISDE ), the Vanderbilt Institute for Nanoscale Science and Engineering ( VINSE ), the Vanderbilt Lab for Immersive AI Translation ( VALIANT ), the Institute for Convergent Software Integrated Systems ( ICONS , formerly known as the Institute for Software Integrated Systems), and the Vanderbilt Institute of National Security ( INS ). Ranked #16 nationally, Vanderbilt University is a private, internationally recognized research university located on 330 park-like acres 1.5 miles from downtown Nashville, Tennessee. Its 10 distinct schools share a single cohesive campus that values collaboration. The university enrolls over 13,800 undergraduate, graduate, and professional students, including 43% minority students and over 1,700 international students from 113 countries. With a metro population of over 2.2 million people, Nashville’s top industries by employment include trade, transportation, and utilities; education and health services; professional and business services; government; and leisure and hospitality. Other industries include manufacturing, financial activities, construction, and information. Long known as a hub for health care and music, Nashville is a technology center with a considerable pool of health care, AI, and defense-related jobs available. In recent years, the city has experienced an influx of major office openings by some of the largest global tech companies and prime Silicon Valley startups. Candidates must have a PhD (or equivalent) in Electrical and Computer Engineering or related field by the time of appointment. The successful candidate will be expected to: · Establish a nationally recognized research program supported by extramural funding · Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in various ECE courses. · Contribute to departmental, school-wide, and university-wide initiatives, fostering a collegial and collaborative environment. Applications should include the following materials: 1. Cover letter 2. Complete curriculum vitae 3. Statement of research interests, highlighting potential collaborative opportunities within and beyond the Defense Tech Cluster 4. Statement of teaching philosophy, including innovative approaches to teaching and curriculum development 5. Service statement 6. Contact information for at least three references"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196501": {
      "title": "Open-Rank Faculty Position- Control Systems & Advanced Manufacturing",
      "description": "Department: Vanderbilt University: School of Engineering: Electrical and Computer Engineering Location: Nashville, TN  Posted: Oct 27, 2026 The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at Vanderbilt University School of Engineering invites applications for an open-ranked, faculty position in Control Systems & Advanced Manufacturing.  This initiative is part of our commitment to establishing a school-wide interdisciplinary Defense Tech Cluster aimed at advancing technologies for national defense and security that can be scaled and translated into products. The successful candidate(s) will have expertise that aligns with our strategic mission to enhance research and education in Electrical and Computer Engineering. The areas of particular focus include s ensors and vision systems, edge computing, digital twins for operational planning, additive manufacturing, modular/open architectures, and methods for rapidly moving technology from prototype to scalable production. The successful candidates will be expected to build research programs that interface with one or several institutes strongly connected with the department and often led by ECE faculty members. These include the recently-formed Vanderbilt Institute for Innovations Beyond Earth ( VIIBE ), the Institute for Space and Defense Electronics ( ISDE ), the Vanderbilt Institute for Nanoscale Science and Engineering ( VINSE ), the Vanderbilt Lab for Immersive AI Translation ( VALIANT ), the Institute for Convergent Software Integrated Systems ( ICONS , formerly known as the Institute for Software Integrated Systems), and the Vanderbilt Institute of National Security ( INS ). Ranked #16 nationally, Vanderbilt University is a private, internationally recognized research university located on 330 park-like acres 1.5 miles from downtown Nashville, Tennessee. Its 10 distinct schools share a single cohesive campus that values collaboration. The university enrolls over 13,800 undergraduate, graduate, and professional students, including 43% minority students and over 1,700 international students from 113 countries. With a metro population of over 2.2 million people, Nashville’s top industries by employment include trade, transportation, and utilities; education and health services; professional and business services; government; and leisure and hospitality. Other industries include manufacturing, financial activities, construction, and information. Long known as a hub for health care and music, Nashville is a technology center with a considerable pool of health care, AI, and defense-related jobs available. In recent years, the city has experienced an influx of major office openings by some of the largest global tech companies and prime Silicon Valley startups. Candidates must have a PhD (or equivalent) in Electrical and Computer Engineering or related field by the time of appointment. The successful candidate will be expected to: · Establish a nationally recognized research program supported by extramural funding · Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in various ECE courses. · Contribute to departmental, school-wide, and university-wide initiatives, fostering a collegial and collaborative environment. Applications should include the following materials: 1. Cover letter 2. Complete curriculum vitae 3. Statement of research interests, highlighting potential collaborative opportunities within and beyond the Defense Tech Cluster 4. Statement of teaching philosophy, including innovative approaches to teaching and curriculum development 5. Service statement 6. Contact information for at least three references"
    },
